      "description": "{\"scope\": [\"Define positive-definite radial kernel interpolation at distinct sites, and conditionally positive-definite augmented system with polynomial side conditions and unisolvent sites.\", \"Prove uniqueness via quadratic form and polynomial unisolvence; define native-space interpolant and power function P_X(x), prove |f(x)-s_X f(x)| <= P_X(x)||f||_H for f in the native space.\", \"For one fixed Wendland or Matérn kernel prove native Sobolev equivalence and fill-distance/separation error estimate on an explicit regular domain; Gaussian flat-shape illconditioning and stable RBF-QR are separate numerical claims, with a defined stable basis algorithm and proved change-of-basis before promising stability.\", \"Define RBF-FD weights by augmented local moment solve; prove polynomial reproduction/local truncation only. PDE stability is owned by PDE consumer.\"], \"proof_route\": \"RKHS projection/Cauchy-Schwarz and saddle-system nullspace argument; Fourier characterization/local zeros lemma is fully local prerequisite; finite moment expansion for RBF-FD.\", \"examples\": [\"Scattered interpolation of smooth surface\", \"Duplicate-site singularity\", \"Small fill distance versus bad separation/conditioning\"]}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-rbf-kernels-sites-and-augmented-interpolation",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Radial kernels, polynomial augmentation, and site geometry",
          "deps": [],
          "justified_by": [
            "lem-rbf-augmented-system-is-invertible"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Narcowich, Ward, and Wendland, Sobolev bounds on functions with scattered zeros, Math. Comp. 74 (2005), §§1 and 3.1",
                "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220577178_Sobolev_bounds_on_functions_with_scattered_zeros_with_applications_to_radial_basis_function_surface_fitting"
              },
              {
                "title": "Rasmussen and Williams, Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning, Chapter 4",
                "url": "https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW4.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$. An admitted domain $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ is a nonempty bounded Lipschitz domain (its boundary is locally the graph of a Lipschitz function, with $\\Omega$ on one side) with fixed $r_0>0$ and $0<\\theta<\\pi/2$ such that for every $x\\in\\overline\\Omega$ there is an orthogonal map $Q_x$ for which\n$$x+Q_x\\{z\\in\\mathbb R^d:0<|z|<r_0,\\ z_1>|z|\\cos\\theta\\}\\subset\\Omega.$$\nAn orthogonal map is a real linear map with $Q_x^{\\mathsf T}Q_x=I$, equivalently one preserving the Euclidean inner product. The map $Q_x$ may depend on $x$; no simultaneous selection of the maps is part of this definition.\n\nA real radial kernel on $\\mathbb R^d$ has the form $K(x,y)=\\phi(|x-y|)$ for a function $\\phi:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$. For a finite ordered list of distinct sites $X=(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$, its Gram matrix is $K_X=(K(x_i,x_j))_{i,j=1}^N$. The kernel is **strictly positive definite** if $c^{\\mathsf T}K_Xc>0$ for every such $X$ and every nonzero $c\\in\\mathbb R^N$; the nonstrict positive-semidefinite condition replaces $>$ by $\\ge$. Only strict positivity is used for the ordinary interpolation solve below.\n\nFor an integer $m\\ge1$, let $\\Pi_{m-1}(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the real polynomials of total degree at most $m-1$, with an ordered basis $p_1,\\ldots,p_J$, where $J=\\dim\\Pi_{m-1}(\\mathbb R^d)$. The evaluation matrix on $X$ is $P_X=(p_j(x_i))_{i,j}$. The sites are **$m$-unisolvent** for this polynomial space when $P_X$ has linearly independent columns. A kernel is **strictly conditionally positive definite of order $m$** when, for every finite distinct $X$ and this evaluation matrix, $c^{\\mathsf T}K_Xc>0$ for every nonzero $c\\in\\mathbb R^N$ satisfying $P_X^{\\mathsf T}c=0$. Thus strict conditional positivity is a strict inequality on the moment-null subspace; it is stronger than the corresponding nonstrict inequality.\n\nFor data $y\\in\\mathbb R^N$, the strictly positive definite interpolation system is $K_Xa=y$. For an order-$m$ strictly conditionally positive definite kernel and $m$-unisolvent sites, the polynomially augmented interpolation system is\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}K_X&P_X\\\\P_X^{\\mathsf T}&0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}a\\\\b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}.$$\nWhenever the relevant system has a unique solution, its kernel-plus-polynomial function $x\\mapsto\\sum_i a_iK(x,x_i)+\\sum_j b_jp_j(x)$ is called the interpolant. Invertibility for the stated strict hypotheses is supplied by [[lem-rbf-augmented-system-is-invertible]].\n\nFor a finite nonempty site set $X\\subset\\Omega$, the **fill distance** is $h_X=\\sup_{x\\in\\Omega}\\min_{x_i\\in X}|x-x_i|$. If $N\\ge2$, the **separation radius** and **mesh ratio** are $q_X=\\tfrac12\\min_{i\\ne j}|x_i-x_j|$ and $\\rho_X=h_X/q_X$, respectively. These are exact geometric quantities, independent of any floating-point solver."
        },
