          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice, and fix $d\\ge1$, $\\nu>0$, $a>0$, $s=\\nu+d/2$, and $A,w,k$ as in [[def-normalized-matern-spectral-kernel]]. Then $w>0$, $w\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^d)$, and\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}w(\\xi)\\,d\\xi=1.$$\nThe integral defining $k(z)$ is absolutely convergent for every $z$; $k$ is real, even, radial, and continuous, and $k(0)=1$. For every $z\\in\\mathbb R^d$,\n$$k(z)=\\frac{a^{2\\nu}}{\\Gamma(\\nu)}\\int_0^\\infty t^{\\nu-1}\\exp\\!\\left(-a^2t-\\frac{|z|^2}{4t}\\right)dt.$$\nFor $z\\ne0$ this is equivalently\n$$k(z)=\\frac{2^{1-\\nu}}{\\Gamma(\\nu)}(a|z|)^\\nu K_\\nu(a|z|),$$\nwhere $K_\\nu$ has the integral normalization in [[def-normalized-matern-spectral-kernel]]. Under the stated Fourier convention, the $d$-dimensional Gaussian identity is, for $t>0$,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}e^{-4\\pi^2t|\\xi|^2}e^{2\\pi i z\\cdot\\xi}\\,d\\xi=(4\\pi t)^{-d/2}e^{-|z|^2/(4t)}.$$"
