              {
                "title": "Narcowich, Ward, and Wendland, Sobolev bounds on functions with scattered zeros, Math. Comp. 74 (2005), §3.1",
                "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220577178_Sobolev_bounds_on_functions_with_scattered_zeros_with_applications_to_radial_basis_function_surface_fitting"
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "The **exact augmented RBF finite-difference weight procedure** has the following inputs: a dimension $d\\ge1$; an integer polynomial degree $r\\ge0$; a finite ordered stencil $Y=(y_1,\\ldots,y_N)\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ of distinct sites that is $(r+1)$-unisolvent for $\\Pi_r(\\mathbb R^d)$; a real kernel $K$ strictly conditionally positive definite of order $r+1$; an ordered basis $p_1,\\ldots,p_J$ of $\\Pi_r(\\mathbb R^d)$; a center $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$; a homogeneous constant-coefficient differential functional $L=\\sum_{|\\alpha|=t}c_\\alpha D^\\alpha$ with $0\\le t\\le r$; and a neighborhood of $x$ on which every section $z\\mapsto K(z,y_i)$ has continuous derivatives through order $t$. Here $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb N_0^d$, $|\\alpha|=\\sum_j\\alpha_j$, and $D^\\alpha$ is the corresponding partial derivative. All arithmetic in this procedure is exact real arithmetic.\n\nIf dimensions, distinctness, unisolvence, the kernel condition, or derivative availability fail, return `inadmissible-input`. Otherwise form $P_{ij}=p_j(y_i)$, $b_i=(L_zK(z,y_i))|_{z=x}$, $\\ell_j=(Lp_j)(x)$, and\n$$M=\\begin{bmatrix}K_Y&P\\\\P^{\\mathsf T}&0\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad g=\\begin{bmatrix}b\\\\ell\\end{bmatrix}.$$\nSolve $Mz=g$ by exact Gaussian elimination with deterministic nonzero-pivot row selection: set $A=M$, $c=g$, and $n=N+J$. For $k=1,\\ldots,n$, choose the least $i\\ge k$ with $A_{ik}\\ne0$; if none exists, return `singular-system`. Swap rows $i$ and $k$ in both $A$ and $c$. For each $j=k+1,\\ldots,n$ in increasing order, set $\\mu=A_{jk}/A_{kk}$, replace row $j$ by row $j-\\mu\\,\\mathrm{row}_k$ in $A$ and $c$, and set $A_{jk}=0$. After all pivots, set $z_n=c_n/A_{nn}$; then, for $k=n-1,n-2,\\ldots,1$ in decreasing order, set $z_k=(c_k-\\sum_{j=k+1}^n A_{kj}z_j)/A_{kk}$. Return `success` with the first $N$ coordinates $w$ of $z$ and the remaining $J$ polynomial multipliers. The stated strict hypotheses imply that `singular-system` is unreachable, as proved by [[lem-rbf-augmented-system-is-invertible]].\n\nThe returned $w$ is an exact mathematical weight vector. The nonsingularity lemma [[lem-rbf-augmented-system-is-invertible]] makes `singular-system` unreachable for every input that passes the stated strict hypotheses. This definition makes no floating-point stability or solver-error claim."
        },
