          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice as a conservative cover of the published $L^2$ completeness dependency, which declares it in its transitive dependencies although its statement requires only Countable Choice. For every $s>d/2$, each $g\\in L^2_s(\\mathbb R^d)$ lies in $L^1(\\mathbb R^d)$, its inverse Fourier representative $U_g$ is bounded and continuous, the map $g\\mapsto U_g$ is injective, and the whole-space Fourier Bessel-potential space of [[def-bessel-potential-space-and-restriction-quotient]] is a complex Hilbert space. In particular, for a constant $C_{d,s}<\\infty$,\n$$|U_g(x)|\\le C_{d,s}\\|g\\|_{L^2_s}\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb R^d).$$"
