    "statement": "Fix the dimension $d\\ge1$ of an admitted domain, a smoothness parameter $\\nu>0$, and an inverse length $a>0$. Assume Countable Choice for the Lebesgue-integration suppliers used to justify this formula; this definition does not assume full Choice. Put $s=\\nu+d/2$ and\n$$A=\\frac{\\Gamma(s)(4\\pi)^{d/2}a^{2\\nu}}{\\Gamma(\\nu)},\\qquad w(\\xi)=A(a^2+4\\pi^2|\\xi|^2)^{-s}.$$\nAll integrals are Lebesgue integrals with respect to $\\lambda_d$ ([[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]), and $\\Gamma$ is the Euler integral of [[def-real-gamma-function-by-the-euler-integral]]. With the negative-sign Fourier transform convention $\\widehat U(\\xi)=\\int U(x)e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}\\,dx$, define the normalized stationary kernel by\n$$k(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}w(\\xi)e^{2\\pi i z\\cdot\\xi}\\,d\\xi,\\qquad K(x,y)=k(x-y).$$\nThe corresponding length parameter in the usual Matérn notation is $\\ell=\\sqrt{2\\nu}/a$. For $b>0$, define the modified Bessel function in its integral normalization by\n$$K_\\nu(b)=\\frac12\\left(\\frac b2\\right)^\\nu\\int_0^\\infty u^{-\\nu-1}e^{-u-b^2/(4u)}\\,du.$$\nThe classical Bessel expression for $k(z)$ when $z\\ne0$, its value $k(0)=1$, absolute convergence, continuity, and radial symmetry are established by [[lem-matern-spectral-integral-and-normalization]]. This definition makes no finite-precision stability claim."
