  {
    "id": "def-tensor-train-cores-bond-ranks-and-canonical-gauges",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Tensor train cores, bond ranks, and canonical gauges",
    "statement": "Let $A\\in\\mathbb F^{n_1\\times\\cdots\\times n_d}$ with $d\\ge2$. A tensor-train (TT) representation consists of integers $r_0=r_d=1$, internal bond sizes $r_1,\\ldots,r_{d-1}\\in\\mathbb N_0$, and cores\n$$ G_k\\in\\mathbb F^{r_{k-1}\\times n_k\\times r_k}\\quad(1\\le k\\le d) $$\nsuch that\n$$ A_{i_1,\\ldots,i_d} =\\sum_{\\alpha_1=1}^{r_1}\\cdots\\sum_{\\alpha_{d-1}=1}^{r_{d-1}} G_1(1,i_1,\\alpha_1)G_2(\\alpha_1,i_2,\\alpha_2)\\cdots G_d(\\alpha_{d-1},i_d,1). $$\nAn empty bond index set gives an empty sum, hence the zero tensor; in particular, all internal bond sizes zero represent zero. A bond size $r_k$ is the storage dimension contracted between cores $k$ and $k+1$. The cut-$k$ unfolding groups modes $1,\\ldots,k$ against $k+1,\\ldots,d$ as fixed in [[def-finite-tensor-arrays-and-frobenius-inner-product]].\n\nFor an internal bond $k$, an invertible matrix $S\\in\\mathbb F^{r_k\\times r_k}$ gives a gauge transformation by replacing the two adjacent cores with\n$$ \\widetilde G_k(\\alpha,i,\\beta)=\\sum_{\\gamma=1}^{r_k}G_k(\\alpha,i,\\gamma)S_{\\gamma\\beta}, \\qquad \\widetilde G_{k+1}(\\beta,j,\\eta)=\\sum_{\\gamma=1}^{r_k}(S^{-1})_{\\beta\\gamma}G_{k+1}(\\gamma,j,\\eta). $$\nTheir contraction is unchanged because $\\sum_\\beta S_{\\gamma\\beta}(S^{-1})_{\\beta\\gamma'}=\\delta_{\\gamma,\\gamma'}$.\n\nThe left matricization of core $G_k$ has row index $(\\alpha_{k-1},i_k)$ and column index $\\alpha_k$. The core is left-isometric if this matrix $L_k$ satisfies $L_k^*L_k=I_{r_k}$. The right matricization has row index $\\alpha_{k-1}$ and column index $(i_k,\\alpha_k)$; the core is right-isometric if its matrix $R_k$ satisfies $R_kR_k^*=I_{r_{k-1}}$. A TT is in mixed canonical gauge about core $\\ell$ when cores $k<\\ell$ are left-isometric and cores $k>\\ell$ are right-isometric.",
    "deps": [
      "def-finite-tensor-arrays-and-frobenius-inner-product"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
