    "statement": "Let $A\\in GL_3(\\mathbb R)$, let $\\Lambda=A\\mathbb Z^3$, let\n$V=|\\det A|$, and let $\\mathbb T_A=\\mathbb R^3/\\Lambda$. These are the\nfull-rank lattice and covolume conventions of\n[[def-full-rank-lattice-covolume-and-dual-lattice]]. The reciprocal lattice is\n$\\Lambda^*=A^{-T}\\mathbb Z^3$. For $k\\in\\mathbb Z^3$ write\n$$\\kappa_k=2\\pi A^{-T}k,\\qquad e_k(x)=e^{i\\kappa_k\\cdot x}.$$\nThus $e_k$ is a character of $\\mathbb T_A$; the ordinary Fourier frequency is\n$A^{-T}k$, while $\\kappa_k$ is the angular physical frequency.\n\nA finite point-source configuration consists of an integer $n\\ge0$, points\n$x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in\\mathbb T_A$ that are pairwise distinct when $n>1$, and\nreal charges $q_1,\\ldots,q_n$. It is **neutral** when\n$\\sum_{i=1}^n q_i=0$. For a smooth periodic test function $\\varphi$, the\npoint-source distribution is the linear functional\n$$\\langle\\rho,\\varphi\\rangle=\\sum_{i=1}^nq_i\\varphi(x_i).$$\nHere smooth means that the periodic lift to $\\mathbb R^3$ has continuous\nderivatives of every order, and the test-function topology is generated by the\nseminorms $\\max_{x\\in A[0,1]^3}|\\partial^\\beta\\varphi(x)|$ over all derivative\nmulti-indices $\\beta$. A periodic distribution is a continuous complex-linear\nfunctional for this topology. Its distributional Laplacian is defined by\n$\\langle-\\Delta u,\\varphi\\rangle=\\langle u,-\\Delta\\varphi\\rangle$.\n\nFor a smooth periodic $\\varphi$, its Fourier coefficient is\n$$\\widehat\\varphi(k)=\\frac1V\\int_{A(0,1]^3}\\varphi(x)e^{-i\\kappa_k\\cdot x}\\,dx.$$\nThe source coefficient is\n$$\\widehat\\rho(k)=\\frac1V\\sum_{i=1}^nq_i e^{-i\\kappa_k\\cdot x_i};$$\nin particular, neutral data have $\\widehat\\rho(0)=0$. For a periodic\ndistribution $u$, define $\\widehat u(k)=V^{-1}\\langle u,e_{-k}\\rangle$. The\n**mean-zero periodic Poisson solution** is a periodic distribution $u$ satisfying\n$-\\Delta u=4\\pi\\rho$ and $\\widehat u(0)=0$. This definition fixes the\nzero-mode convention; no nonneutral periodic solution is implicit.\n\nThe Coulomb energy convention is the half-sum of each source charge times the\nregular part of its potential, after subtracting its own singular term\n$q_i/|x-x_i|$. The resulting finite part is called the **renormalized\nself-energy convention**. Its Ewald formula is established in this development."
  },
