{
      "id": "oscillatory-filon-and-levin-quadrature",
      "kind": "A",
      "category": "numerical-linear-algebra-and-approximation",
      "label": "QUAD-OSC",
      "title": "Oscillatory integrals and filon levin methods",
      "companion": "oscillatory-filon-and-levin-quadrature-examples",
      "requires": [
        "composite-quadrature-rules",
        "finite-precision-direct-factorizations",
        "outward-rounded-interval-and-ball-arithmetic",
        "smooth-partitions-of-unity-and-exhaustions"
      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": [\"Define Filon-type interpolation of amplitude for integral f(x) exp(i omega g(x)) dx on finite interval with omega!=0 and nonstationary g prime bounded away0; exact oscillatory moments are an explicitly computed supplier.\", \"Prove repeated integration-by-parts endpoint expansion and remainder O(|omega|^-r) with constants from derivatives of f/g prime; stationary points require separate subdivision/local stationary-phase expansion.\", \"Define Levin collocation p prime+i omega g prime p=f and endpoint formula; prove residual integral bounds using same nonstationary estimates and condition of collocation solve, not residual size alone.\", \"Add simple nondegenerate stationary-point quadratic scaling with Fresnel moment/remainder and declared smoothness; endpoint stationary and coalescing cases need separate Airy/uniform treatment, assigned here if consumed.\"], \"proof_route\": \"Explicit derivative recurrence for oscillatory IBP; polynomial exact moments; ODE endpoint identity; local Morse coordinate with controlled remainder and Fresnel tail.\", \"examples\": [\"Large-frequency Fourier integral\", \"Interior stationary phase missed by nonstationary bound\", \"Levin residual plus illconditioned solve\"]}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-na-finite-interval-oscillatory-integral",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite-interval oscillatory integral and phase convention",
          "deps": [
            "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
            "thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives",
            "cor-differentiable-implies-continuous",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Let $a<b$, $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C$ be continuous, and $g:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. Define\n$$I_\\omega(f,g;[a,b]) := \\int_a^b f(x)e^{i\\omega g(x)}\\,dx.$$\nThe complex Riemann integral here means the real Riemann integral of the real part plus $i$ times the real Riemann integral of the imaginary part. Euler's formula [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]] and continuity of sine and cosine, which follows from [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]] and differentiability implying continuity [[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]], make $e^{i\\omega g}$ continuous. The product is continuous, so both component integrals exist by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]. A function is in $C^r[a,b]$ when it and its derivatives through order $r$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, with one-sided endpoint derivatives. A phase is nonstationary on $[a,b]$ when $g\\in C^1[a,b]$ and $|g'(x)|\\ge c$ everywhere for some fixed $c>0$. For such a phase and differentiable $h$, put $T_g^0h=h$ and $T_g^{j+1}h=(T_g^jh/g')'$ whenever that derivative exists.\n\nThis is an exact-real definition. A later numerical implementation must separately state its moment errors and any floating-point solve error.",
          "strategy": "Let $a<b$, $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C$ be continuous, and $g:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. Define\n$$I_\\omega(f,g;[a,b]) := \\int_a^b f(x)e^{i\\omega g(x)}\\,dx.$$\nThe complex Riemann integral here means the real Riemann integral of the real part plus $i$ times the real Riemann integral of the imaginary part. Euler's formula [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]] and continuity of sine and cosine, which follows from [[thm-sine-and-cosine-derivatives]] and differentiability implying continuity [[cor-differentiable-implies-continuous]], make $e^{i\\omega g}$ continuous. The product is continuous, so both component integrals exist by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]. A function is in $C^r[a,b]$ when it and its derivatives through order $r$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, with one-sided endpoint derivatives. A phase is nonstationary on $[a,b]$ when $g\\in C^1[a,b]$ and $|g'(x)|\\ge c$ everywhere for some fixed $c>0$. For such a phase and differentiable $h$, put $T_g^0h=h$ and $T_g^{j+1}h=(T_g^jh/g')'$ whenever that derivative exists.\n\nThis is an exact-real definition. A later numerical implementation must separately state its moment errors and any floating-point solve error.",
