          "dependency_level": 5,
          "statement": "Let $a<b$, $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$, $f\\in C^2[a,b]$ be complex-valued, and $g\\in C^3[a,b]$ be real-valued with $|g'|\\ge c>0$. Use the endpoint Lagrange rule from [[alg-na-filon-nodal-moment-quadrature]] and put $r=f-p_n$. If $\\mu_j=\\int_a^b\\ell_j e^{i\\omega g}$ and the rule uses $\\widehat\\mu_j$ with $|\\widehat\\mu_j-\\mu_j|\\le\\epsilon_j$, then\n$$|I_\\omega(f,g;[a,b])-Q_n|\\le\\frac{|T_gr(a)/g'(a)|+|T_gr(b)/g'(b)|+(b-a)\\|T_g^2r\\|_\\infty}{|\\omega|^2}+\\eta_n.$$\nWith exact moments $\\eta_n=0$, this is an $O(|\\omega|^{-2})$ endpoint-interpolation error bound. The constant depends on the fixed amplitude, phase, nodes, and derivative norms.",
          "strategy": "direct\n\n**Given:** The hypotheses in the Statement, the integral convention on [[def-na-finite-interval-oscillatory-integral]], and the endpoint nodes $c_0=a,c_n=b$.\n\n[F1] [[lem-na-lagrange-interpolation-on-distinct-nodes]]: $p_n=\\sum_{j=0}^nf(c_j)\\ell_j$ and $p_n(c_j)=f(c_j)$.\n\n[F2] [[alg-na-filon-nodal-moment-quadrature]]: $Q_n=\\sum_jf(c_j)\\widehat\\mu_j$ and $\\eta_n=\\sum_j|f(c_j)|\\epsilon_j$.\n\n[F3] [[thm-na-nonstationary-oscillatory-integration-by-parts]]: the two-step identity for $I_\\omega(h,g)$ has first boundary term $[(h/g')e^{i\\omega g}]_a^b/(i\\omega)$, second term $-[(T_gh/g')e^{i\\omega g}]_a^b/(i\\omega)^2$, and remainder $I_\\omega(T_g^2h,g)/(i\\omega)^2$.\n\n[F4] The complex modulus is subadditive, $|z+w|\\le|z|+|w|$ ([[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]]).\n\n[F5] Real Riemann integrals on an interval are linear by [[thm-multidimensional-integral-properties]] with $m=1$; the componentwise complex definition [[def-na-finite-interval-oscillatory-integral]] extends this to complex integrands.\n\n1.1 Let $Q_n^\\ast=\\sum_jf(c_j)\\mu_j$. By [F1] and [F5], linearity of the integral gives $Q_n^\\ast=I_\\omega(p_n,g)$; by [F2] and the finite-sum triangle inequality [F4], $|Q_n^\\ast-Q_n|\\le\\eta_n$. The same linearity gives $I_\\omega(f,g)-Q_n=I_\\omega(r,g)+(Q_n^\\ast-Q_n)$. [given, F1, F2, F4, F5, algebra]\n\n1.2 The endpoint nodes and [F1] give $r(a)=r(b)=0$, so the first boundary term in [F3] vanishes. Applying [F3] to $r$ leaves $I_\\omega(r,g)=-\\frac{[(T_gr/g')e^{i\\omega g}]_a^b}{(i\\omega)^2}+\\frac{I_\\omega(T_g^2r,g)}{(i\\omega)^2}.$ [given, F1, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 Since $|e^{i\\omega g}|=1$, the complex triangle inequality [F4] bounds the displayed boundary term by $|T_gr(a)/g'(a)|+|T_gr(b)/g'(b)|$, and the integral term is at most $(b-a)\\|T_g^2r\\|_\\infty$ by [F3]. Combine these bounds with step 1.1 and $|(i\\omega)^2|=|\\omega|^2$ using [F4] to obtain the assertion. [given, F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎",
