  {
    "id": "lem-na-lagrange-interpolation-on-distinct-nodes",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "Lagrange interpolation at distinct real nodes",
    "deps": [
      "thm-nonzero-real-polynomial-has-at-most-degree-many-distinct-roots"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $c_0,\\ldots,c_n$ be distinct real numbers. For arbitrary values $y_0,\\ldots,y_n\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$\\ell_j(x)=\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le k\\le n\\\\k\\ne j}}\\frac{x-c_k}{c_j-c_k},\\qquad p(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^n y_j\\ell_j(x).$$\nThen $p$ is the unique polynomial of degree at most $n$ satisfying $p(c_j)=y_j$. In particular, interpolation reproduces every polynomial of degree at most $n$.",
    "strategy": "direct\n\n**Given:** The stated distinct nodes and complex data.\n\n[F1] [[thm-nonzero-real-polynomial-has-at-most-degree-many-distinct-roots]]: a nonzero real polynomial of degree at most $n$ has at most $n$ distinct real roots. Applied to real and imaginary parts, the same root bound holds for complex polynomials.\n\n1.1 Since $c_j-c_k\\ne0$ for $j\\ne k$, every denominator in $\\ell_j$ is nonzero. At $x=c_m$, the factor for $k=m$ makes $\\ell_j(c_m)=0$ when $j\\ne m$, while every factor in $\\ell_m(c_m)$ equals $1$; hence $\\ell_j(c_m)=1$ exactly when $j=m$. The displayed $p$ has degree at most $n$ and $p(c_m)=y_m$. [given, construct, algebra]\n\n2.1 If $q$ is another such polynomial, then $q-p$ has degree at most $n$ and vanishes at the $n+1$ distinct nodes. Its real and imaginary parts each have those roots, so [F1] forces both parts to be zero polynomials. Thus $q=p$. If the data are values of a polynomial of degree at most $n$, uniqueness identifies that polynomial with $p$. [given, F1, algebra] ∎",
