    "statement": "Input $a<b$, $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$, continuous $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb C$, continuous real $g:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$, an integer $n\\ge1$, distinct nodes $a=c_0<\\cdots<c_n=b$, and for each Lagrange basis polynomial $\\ell_j$ a complex moment estimate $\\widehat\\mu_j\\in\\mathbb C$ with real error bound $\\epsilon_j\\ge0$ satisfying $|\\widehat\\mu_j-\\int_a^b\\ell_j(x)e^{i\\omega g(x)}dx|\\le\\epsilon_j$. Put $f_j=f(c_j)$. Form $p_n=\\sum_{j=0}^nf_j\\ell_j$ using [[lem-na-lagrange-interpolation-on-distinct-nodes]], then return\n$$Q_n=\\sum_{j=0}^nf_j\\widehat\\mu_j,\\qquad \\eta_n=\\sum_{j=0}^n|f_j|\\epsilon_j.$$\nFor $g(x)=x$, expand each real-node Lagrange polynomial as $\\ell_j(x)=\\sum_{k=0}^n a_{jk}x^k$, run [[alg-na-fourier-polynomial-moments]] through $k=n$, and set $\\widehat\\mu_j=\\sum_{k=0}^na_{jk}\\mu_k$ and $\\epsilon_j=0$. For general nonstationary $g$, a user may obtain bounded moments by applying [[alg-na-nonstationary-moment-expansion]] to each $\\ell_j$ with a certified derivative bound. The exact-moment branch for a general phase is conditional on supplied exact moments; no general exact moment solver for arbitrary nonlinear $g$ is asserted here.",
