      "description": "{\"scope\": [\"Define planar finite point set, triangulation, empty-circle Delaunay and Voronoi dual, duplicates/collinear/cocircular cases and hull treatment.\", \"Define incremental insertion plus exact-predicate edge flips with deterministic cocircular convention; prove invariant, termination by lifted paraboloid functional and empty-circle correctness.\", \"Prove planar O(n) triangulation size by Euler count and expected O(n log n) randomized incremental location only with explicit uniform random permutation/history DAG assumptions; worst-case cost stated separately.\", \"Define barycentric point location/interpolation and nearest-neighbor Voronoi query with outside-hull status; no guaranteed mesh minimum angle without quality refinement proof from PDE owner.\", \"Add dimension3 Delaunay tetrahedralization and weighted regular triangulation via lifted convex hull with exact orientation/insphere or power predicate, finite insertion/cavity repair and degeneracy statuses. Prove geometric coverage/empty-sphere invariant and finite termination with lexicographic lifting; worst-case output size O(n²), not planar O(n). Tetrahedral quality/sliver elimination requires separate PDE-owned refinement theorem; Delaunay alone gives no minimum dihedral bound.\"], \"proof_route\": \"Exact orientation/incircle, convex lifted hull identity, flip potential and planar Euler induction; backward analysis for expected insertion work and location structure.\", \"examples\": [\"Cocircular four-point tie\", \"Collinear input lower-dimensional output\", \"Scattered linear interpolant over Delaunay cells\"]}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite indexed geometric site sets and duplicate handling",
          "deps": [],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Throughout this page, $\\mathbb R$ is a commutative field with $1>0$, a total order compatible with addition and multiplication, and the least-upper-bound property. Let $\\mathbb D=\\{m2^e:m,e\\in\\mathbb Z\\}$ be its dyadic subfield. For $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$, a nonempty finite indexed site list is a finite list\n$$S=((\\iota_1,p_1,w_1),\\ldots,(\\iota_N,p_N,w_N)),$$\nwhere every $\\iota_i\\in\\mathbb Z$ is a distinct stable identifier, every $p_i\\in\\mathbb D^d$, and each optional weight $w_i$ is either absent (meaning $0$) or belongs to $\\mathbb D$. In unweighted mode, a duplicate means equal coordinates; collapse each such class to its least identifier. In weighted mode, a duplicate means an equal coordinate-weight pair; collapse only equal pairs to the least identifier. Equal coordinates with unequal weights remain distinct weighted sites.\n\nThe unweighted deduplicated set keeps, for each coordinate value $p$, the entry with least identifier among entries at $p$, and records each discarded identifier in a duplicate map. The weighted deduplicated set keeps the least identifier for each exact pair $(p,w)$ and records only repeated pairs in its duplicate map. A weighted computation retains different weights at the same coordinate; their power values are compared by the ordinary power-distance rule, so a dominated weight may be hidden.\n\nFor a finite point set $P\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, its convex hull is the set of all finite convex combinations of points of $P$. Its affine dimension is the dimension of its affine hull. A tuple $p_0,\\ldots,p_d$ is affinely independent when the vectors $p_1-p_0,\\ldots,p_d-p_0$ form a basis of $\\mathbb R^d$; here a basis means every vector has a unique linear expansion in those vectors. This convention makes nondegenerate simplex coordinates unique.",
          "source_path": "items/def-indexed-geometric-site-set.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
