        },
        {
          "id": "lem-linear-cardinal-interpolant-on-an-edge",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "The affine edge interpolant is the unique degree-one cardinal polynomial",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Let $F$ be a field, let $a,b\\in F$ with $a\\ne b$, and let $y_0,y_1\\in F$. The unique polynomial $P\\in F[X]$ of degree at most one with $P(a)=y_0$ and $P(b)=y_1$ is\n$$P(X)=y_0+(y_1-y_0)\\frac{X-a}{b-a}.$$",
          "source_path": "items/lem-linear-cardinal-interpolant-on-an-edge.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://ics-websites.science.uu.nl/geobook/interpolation.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Stable-id lexicographic lift perturbation for cocircular and power ties",
          "deps": [
            "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
            "def-certified-geometric-predicate-determinants"
          ],
          "justified_by": [
            "thm-lexicographic-lift-tie-break-consistent"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Take a fixed finite universe $U$ of indexed sites as in [[def-indexed-geometric-site-set]], dimension $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$, and an ordered tuple of $d+2$ sites from $U$ with distinct identifiers. A computation using auxiliary sites, such as a planar supertriangle, includes those sites in $U$ from the start and assigns them distinct deterministic integer identifiers. Require that the first $d+1$ sites are affinely independent. Put $h_i=\\|p_i\\|^2-w_i$, with absent weights interpreted as $0$. Rank all identifiers of $U$ in increasing order, starting at rank $0$, and define\n$$P(\\varepsilon)=\\det\\bigl((p_i,h_i+\\varepsilon^{2^{r_i}},1)\\bigr)_{i=0}^{d+1}.$$\nRegard $\\varepsilon$ as a positive formal infinitesimal in the rational-function field $\\mathbb R(\\varepsilon)$, ordered so a nonzero polynomial has the sign of its coefficient at its least nonzero exponent. Expand this finite polynomial exactly and return the sign of its coefficient at the least exponent with nonzero coefficient. If the base is degenerate, return status degenerate-base. The same universe $U$ and ranks are used for every predicate tuple. The procedure never chooses or evaluates a numerical $\\varepsilon$; its returned sign is the declared stable-id tie convention for the raw lifted determinant in [[def-certified-geometric-predicate-determinants]].",
          "source_path": "items/def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Don Sheehy, Computational Geometry, Fall 2014, Lecture 22: Weighted Delaunay Triangulations",
                "url": "https://donsheehy.net/courses/comp_geom/2014_fall/notes/22_weighted_delaunay.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "CGAL 6.3 User Manual, 3D Triangulations, Regular Triangulation",
                "url": "https://cgal.github.io/9465/doc/Triangulation_3/index.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-lifted-power-sphere-determinant-identity",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "The lifted determinant records a power-sphere side",
          "deps": [
            "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
            "def-certified-geometric-predicate-determinants"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Let $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$, let $p_0,\\ldots,p_d,q\\in\\mathbb D^d$, and let their dyadic weights be $w_0,\\ldots,w_d,w_q$ (zero when absent). Assume $p_0,\\ldots,p_d$ are affinely independent. If $c\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $R\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy\n$$\\|p_i-c\\|^2-w_i=R\\qquad(0\\le i\\le d),$$\ndefine the power difference\n$$\\Delta(q)=\\|q-c\\|^2-w_q-R.$$\nLet $O_d$ be the determinant of the rows $(p_i,1)$ for $i=0,\\ldots,d$, and let $P_d$ be the determinant of the $d+2$ rows $(p_i,\\|p_i\\|^2-w_i,1)$, including the query row $(q,\\|q\\|^2-w_q,1)$. Then\n$$P_d=-O_d\\Delta(q),\\qquad O_dP_d=-O_d^2\\Delta(q).$$\nThus the sign of $O_dP_d$ is positive, zero or negative exactly when $q$ is respectively inside, on or outside the power sphere. When all weights are zero, this is the ordinary circumsphere convention.",
          "source_path": "items/lem-lifted-power-sphere-determinant-identity.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David M. Mount, CMSC 754 Computational Geometry, Lecture 13: Delaunay Triangulations: Incremental Construction",
                "url": "https://jeffe.web.engr.illinois.edu/teaching/compgeom/2021/refs/Mount.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Don Sheehy, Computational Geometry, Fall 2014, Lecture 22: Weighted Delaunay Triangulations",
                "url": "https://donsheehy.net/courses/comp_geom/2014_fall/notes/22_weighted_delaunay.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "CGAL 6.3 User Manual, 3D Triangulations, Regular Triangulation",
                "url": "https://cgal.github.io/9465/doc/Triangulation_3/index.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-voronoi-vertex-empty-circle",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "A Voronoi vertex has a common empty circle of nearest sites",
          "deps": [
            "def-indexed-geometric-site-set"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Let $P\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ be finite and nonempty, and define $V(p)=\\{x:\\|x-p\\|^2\\le\\|x-q\\|^2\\ \\text{for all }q\\in P\\}$. If $x$ belongs to at least three distinct cells, then all sites whose cells contain $x$ lie on one circle centered at $x$, no site lies strictly inside that circle, and their convex hull is a polygon with those sites as its vertices.",
          "source_path": "items/lem-voronoi-vertex-empty-circle.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "David M. Mount, CMSC 754 Computational Geometry, Lecture 11",
                "url": "https://jeffe.web.engr.illinois.edu/teaching/compgeom/2021/refs/Mount.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://ics-websites.science.uu.nl/geobook/interpolation.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-planar-delaunay-voronoi-structures",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Planar triangulations, Delaunay structures, and Voronoi cells",
          "deps": [
            "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
            "def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break",
            "lem-voronoi-vertex-empty-circle"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "Use the unweighted deduplicated coordinate set of [[def-indexed-geometric-site-set]]. If its affine dimension is zero, its output is its single site. If its affine dimension is one, sort by the first varying coordinate (use $x$ if it varies, otherwise $y$) and take the chain joining consecutive sites; this is the lower-dimensional output and contains no triangles.\n\nFor affine dimension two, a **triangulation of the sites** is a finite family of nondegenerate closed triangles whose vertices are sites, whose union is the convex hull, and whose pairwise intersections are common faces. Its 1-skeleton is the graph with these sites as vertices and all triangle sides as edges; it is connected. The bounded faces of this plane graph are exactly the chosen triangles, and its one unbounded face is the exterior of the convex hull. The boundary edges of the convex hull are subdivided at every site on that boundary. A triangle is **weakly empty-circle** when no site lies strictly inside its circumdisk; other sites may lie on its boundary.\n\nFor each site $p$, its closed **Voronoi cell** is\n$$V(p)=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:\\|x-p\\|^2\\le\\|x-q\\|^2\\text{ for every site }q\\}.$$\nA tie belongs to every minimizing cell. The **Delaunay graph** has one vertex for each site and an edge $pq$ exactly when $V(p)\\cap V(q)$ contains a nontrivial line segment. A **Voronoi vertex** is a point belonging to at least three distinct cells. It is dual to the convex polygon of the nearest sites; [[lem-voronoi-vertex-empty-circle]] proves that those sites are vertices on one empty circle centered at that point. A **Delaunay triangulation** is a triangulation refining these polygonal Delaunay faces and having weakly empty circumdisks. In a cocircular face the triangulation need not be unique; the chosen algorithmic refinement is the fixed-universe convention of [[def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break]].\n\nThe convex-hull boundary is the boundary of the union of the triangles. An unbounded Voronoi cell corresponds to a site on that boundary. No minimum-angle or other mesh-quality condition is part of these definitions.",
          "source_path": "items/def-planar-delaunay-voronoi-structures.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://ics-websites.science.uu.nl/geobook/interpolation.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David M. Mount, CMSC 754 Computational Geometry, Lectures 11–13",
                "url": "https://jeffe.web.engr.illinois.edu/teaching/compgeom/2021/refs/Mount.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-three-dimensional-delaunay-and-weighted-regular-lower-hull",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Three-dimensional Delaunay and weighted regular triangulations by lifting",
          "deps": [
            "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
            "lem-lifted-power-sphere-determinant-identity",
            "def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "For a weighted site $(p,w)$, define its power distance from $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ by\n$$\\pi_{(p,w)}(x)=\\|x-p\\|^2-w.$$\nIts lifted point is $\\widehat p=(p,\\|p\\|^2-w)\\in\\mathbb R^4$. A lower face of the lifted convex hull is a face supported by a nonvertical hyperplane $z=\\ell(x)$ with every lifted site on or above that hyperplane. The projection of the union of lower faces is the **regular subdivision** of the projected sites; a simplex in that subdivision has an empty power sphere, meaning its common power distance is no larger than that of any input site.\n\nThe lex-refined lift is interpreted over the ordered rational-function field of [[def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break]], using one fixed finite universe of all input sites. When every weight is zero, the regular subdivision is the ordinary 3D Delaunay subdivision and power spheres are circumspheres. A weight configuration can hide a site: a weighted site is **hidden** if its lift is not a vertex of any lower face. A nonsimplicial lower face is refined with the same fixed-universe lexicographic lift convention. For projected affine dimension below three, the algorithm reports `lower-dimensional` with that dimension and the exact deduplicated site list, and makes no tetrahedralization claim. A tetrahedral output is claimed only on the full-dimensional branch with nondegenerate projected bases.",
          "source_path": "items/def-three-dimensional-delaunay-and-weighted-regular-lower-hull.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Don Sheehy, Computational Geometry, Fall 2014, Lecture 22: Weighted Delaunay Triangulations",
                "url": "https://donsheehy.net/courses/comp_geom/2014_fall/notes/22_weighted_delaunay.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "CGAL 6.3 User Manual, 3D Triangulations, Regular Triangulation",
                "url": "https://cgal.github.io/9465/doc/Triangulation_3/index.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-lexicographic-lift-tie-break-consistent",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "The finite lexicographic lift tie-break is coherent",
          "deps": [
            "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
            "def-certified-geometric-predicate-determinants",
            "lem-lifted-power-sphere-determinant-identity",
            "def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "statement": "Fix one finite universe $U$ of uniquely indexed dyadic sites and weights and a dimension $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$. For an ordered tuple of $d+2$ distinct sites whose first $d+1$ sites are affinely independent, the lexicographic lift procedure in [[def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break]] is the determinant sign of one common formal lifted configuration. Every exact nonzero power determinant retains its original sign, every exact zero has a nonzero polynomial tie determinant, and all tuples therefore receive mutually coherent signs. No numeric perturbation parameter is selected or assumed.",
          "source_path": "items/thm-lexicographic-lift-tie-break-consistent.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Don Sheehy, Computational Geometry, Fall 2014, Lecture 22: Weighted Delaunay Triangulations",
                "url": "https://donsheehy.net/courses/comp_geom/2014_fall/notes/22_weighted_delaunay.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "CGAL 6.3 User Manual, 3D Triangulations, Regular Triangulation",
                "url": "https://cgal.github.io/9465/doc/Triangulation_3/index.html"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-barycentric-point-location-interpolation-and-voronoi-query",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Barycentric point location, affine interpolation, and nearest-site queries",
          "deps": [
            "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
            "def-planar-delaunay-voronoi-structures"
          ],
          "justified_by": [
            "thm-barycentric-interpolation-and-nearest-neighbor-query-correct"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "Let $T$ be a nondegenerate triangle with ordered vertices $p_0,p_1,p_2\\in\\mathbb D^2$. For $x\\in\\mathbb R^2$, its **barycentric coordinates** are the unique scalars $\\lambda_0,\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2$ satisfying $\\sum_i\\lambda_i=1$ and $x=\\sum_i\\lambda_i p_i$. They are exact for dyadic $x$: they are obtained by solving the invertible $2\\times2$ system with columns $p_1-p_0,p_2-p_0$. The point is in $T$ exactly when all $\\lambda_i\\ge0$.\n\nGiven vertex data $f_0,f_1,f_2\\in\\mathbb R$, the **barycentric affine interpolant** on $T$ is $I_Tf(x)=\\sum_i\\lambda_i f_i$. A point-location result is a containing triangle of the triangulation, with a shared-edge or shared-vertex result allowed when the point lies on a face; outside the convex hull its status is `outside-hull` and no containing triangle is returned.\n\nFor a finite unweighted site set $P$, the **nearest-site query** returns a site minimizing $\\|x-p\\|^2$, breaking ties by the least stable identifier. Its hull status is `inside-hull` for $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}P$ and `outside-hull` otherwise. The minimizer is returned in either case.",
          "source_path": "items/def-barycentric-point-location-interpolation-and-voronoi-query.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://ics-websites.science.uu.nl/geobook/interpolation.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David M. Mount, CMSC 754 Computational Geometry, Lectures 11–13",
                "url": "https://jeffe.web.engr.illinois.edu/teaching/compgeom/2021/refs/Mount.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-euler-count-for-planar-triangulation",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Euler counting for a finite planar triangulation",
          "deps": [
            "def-planar-delaunay-voronoi-structures"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "For a finite triangulation of the convex hull of $n$ distinct planar sites of affine dimension two, let $k$ be the number of sites on the hull boundary, $E$ the number of edges, and $T$ the number of bounded triangular faces. Then\n$$3T=2E-k,\\qquad n-E+T=1,$$\nand consequently $T=2n-2-k$ and $E=3n-3-k$.",
          "source_path": "items/lem-euler-count-for-planar-triangulation.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9",
                "url": "https://ics-websites.science.uu.nl/geobook/interpolation.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David M. Mount, CMSC 754 Computational Geometry, Lecture 12",
                "url": "https://jeffe.web.engr.illinois.edu/teaching/compgeom/2021/refs/Mount.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-large-delaunay-supertriangle-exists",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "A dyadic supertriangle contains the sites and excludes every input circumcircle",
          "deps": [
            "def-indexed-geometric-site-set",
            "def-planar-delaunay-voronoi-structures",
            "lem-complete-ordered-field-is-archimedean"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "For every finite full-dimensional dyadic planar site set, a dyadic supertriangle can be chosen to contain every input site strictly and to have all three vertices strictly outside every circumcircle through a noncollinear input triple. If the input has no three collinear and no four cocircular sites, the supertriangle can additionally be chosen so the augmented set has no three collinear sites and no four cocircular sites.",
          "source_path": "items/lem-large-delaunay-supertriangle-exists.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9, Sections 9.2–9.3",
                "url": "https://ics-websites.science.uu.nl/geobook/interpolation.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David M. Mount, CMSC 754 Computational Geometry, Lecture 13",
                "url": "https://jeffe.web.engr.illinois.edu/teaching/compgeom/2021/refs/Mount.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-lifted-flip-potential-decreases",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "An illegal planar Delaunay flip strictly decreases lifted interpolation volume",
          "deps": [
            "def-planar-delaunay-voronoi-structures",
            "lem-lifted-power-sphere-determinant-identity",
            "def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break",
            "thm-lexicographic-lift-tie-break-consistent"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "Let $ABCD$ be a strictly convex planar quadrilateral in counterclockwise boundary order. Lift its four sites using the common formal heights of [[def-lexicographic-lift-tie-break]]. Let $g_{AC}$ and $g_{BD}$ be the piecewise-affine interpolants on the two diagonal triangulations. Define each interpolant's integral over the quadrilateral as the sum over its two triangles of triangle area times the average of the three vertex heights. If the lifted point $D$ lies strictly below the affine plane through the lifts of $A,B,C$ (the illegal-incircle sign by the coefficientwise determinant identity), then\n$$\\int_{ABCD}g_{BD}<\\int_{ABCD}g_{AC}$$\nin the lexicographic order of the formal lift field.",
          "source_path": "items/lem-lifted-flip-potential-decreases.md",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mark de Berg et al., Computational Geometry: Algorithms and Applications, 3rd ed., Chapter 9, Section 9.2",
                "url": "https://ics-websites.science.uu.nl/geobook/interpolation.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "David M. Mount, CMSC 754 Computational Geometry, Lecture 13",
                "url": "https://jeffe.web.engr.illinois.edu/teaching/compgeom/2021/refs/Mount.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Don Sheehy, Computational Geometry, Fall 2014, Lecture 22",
                "url": "https://donsheehy.net/courses/comp_geom/2014_fall/notes/22_weighted_delaunay.pdf"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-lower-lifted-facets-cover-the-convex-hull",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Generic lower lifted facets project to a triangulation of the convex hull",
          "deps": [
