    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Skeleton and projected least-squares CUR cores",
    "statement": "Let $A\\in\\mathbb F^{m\\times n}$ over $\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ with $m,n\\ge1$, and let $I=(i_1,\\ldots,i_k)$ and $J=(j_1,\\ldots,j_k)$ be ordered lists of distinct row and column indices. For $k=0$, both lists and all selected matrices have zero size, with ordinary empty-matrix multiplication. Define $C=A(:,J)\\in\\mathbb F^{m\\times k}$, $R=A(I,:)\\in\\mathbb F^{k\\times n}$, and the cross matrix $W=A(I,J)\\in\\mathbb F^{k\\times k}$, using the same orders in $I,J,W$.\n\nThe **skeleton CUR core** is $U_{\\mathrm{skel}}=W^\\dagger$ and its reconstruction is $\\widehat A_{\\mathrm{skel}}=C W^\\dagger R$. The **projected least-squares CUR core** is $U_{\\mathrm{proj}}=C^\\dagger A R^\\dagger$ and its reconstruction is\n$\\widehat A_{\\mathrm{proj}}=C(C^\\dagger A R^\\dagger)R=(CC^\\dagger)A(R^\\dagger R)$.\n\nFor $k=0$, both reconstructions are the zero $m\\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe Moore-Penrose theorem in the dependencies guarantees that these formulas are single-valued for singular as well as nonsingular $C,R,W$. The two core constructions may yield the same matrix: for $A=[1]$ and $I=J=(1)$, both cores equal $[1]$. The projection residual bound on the A page applies only to $\\widehat A_{\\mathrm{proj}}$; it is not asserted for the skeleton core $W^\\dagger$.",
