    "title": "Degree-indexed spherical harmonic spaces in three dimensions",
    "statement": "Let $\\mathbb S^2=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:|x|=1\\}$ with its usual surface-area measure $dS$, normalized so $\\int_{\\mathbb S^2}dS=4\\pi$. For an integer $\\ell\\ge0$, let $\\mathcal H_\\ell$ be the complex vector space of restrictions to $\\mathbb S^2$ of homogeneous degree-$\\ell$ polynomials $P(x_1,x_2,x_3)$ satisfying $\\Delta P=0$. Give $\\mathcal H_\\ell$ the inner product $\\langle f,g\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb S^2}f\\overline g\\,dS$, linear in the first argument. An orthonormal basis is not fixed by this definition; the addition-theorem item proves that $\\dim\\mathcal H_\\ell=2\\ell+1$ and that such a finite basis exists.\n\nIf $Y_{\\ell m}\\in\\mathcal H_\\ell$ is later chosen from an orthonormal basis and $v\\ne0$, its associated homogeneous harmonic polynomial is $H_{\\ell m}(v)=|v|^\\ell Y_{\\ell m}(v/|v|)$. At $v=0$ use the polynomial value: $H_{00}(0)=Y_{00}$ and $H_{\\ell m}(0)=0$ for $\\ell>0$. This is the regular-solid-harmonic convention used in the 3D evaluator.",
