del without a separate structured perturbation proof.\", \"Prove innovation triangular-solve residual/forward bounds with sigma_min(L11)>0, and perturbation bounds for the block Schur complement/conditional mean retaining ||S^-1||, ||Pminus H*|| and data scale. Positive innovation lower bound must dominate joint perturbation to identify nearby covariance update. Finite-horizon propagation sums per-step factor/product/solve errors with explicitly derived Lipschitz factors; no uniform-in-time stability or accuracy follows without detectability/stability/conditioning assumptions proved here if advertised.\", \"Compare the canonical subtractive update and existing Joseph identity only as algebraic context: numerical cancellation can make a directly rounded Pminus-KSK* indefinite, whereas L22 product is PSD. Do not promise a stored rounded covariance matrix formed from L22 is PSD in exact represented entries unless formed/rounded with an additional certified symmetric factor interpretation. Posterior statistics remain inherited with the original AC assumptions; numerical matrix identities alone require no new choice.\"], \"proof_route\": \"Use canonical one-step block covariance and Gaussian/Joseph supplier. Gram-matrix block multiplication plus QR gives exact numerical equivalence, including semidefinite rank loss. Use NA-02 exact orthogonal representative/backward QR error to obtain a nearby prearray, then algebraic Gram perturbation and Schur-complement resolvent identity. Triangular residual bounds and explicitly conditioned finite-horizon recursion supply actual error guarantees, with input uncertainty and arithmetic error separated.\", \"examples\": [\"Canonical ex-kalman-filter-singular-prior-positive-innovation rerun through rectangular square roots without new statistical example claim\", \"Nearly noiseless observation amplifies innovation solve error\", \"Two-scale covariance where subtractive floating update loses PSD\", \"Nonzero factor residual and PSD represented L22 product with perturbed joint covariance\"]}",
