            "thm-termwise-differentiation-of-complex-power-series"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Airy coefficient recurrence and geometric tail bounds",
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "DLMF, §9.4, Airy Maclaurin series",
                "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/9.4"
              },
              {
                "title": "DLMF, §9.2(ii), Airy initial values",
                "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/9.2"
              },
              {
                "title": "DLMF, §§9.2(ii), 9.4, and 9.17(i)",
                "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/9.17"
              },
              {
                "title": "N. M. Temme, “Numerical aspects of special functions,” Acta Numerica 17 (2008), §2",
                "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/11612/11612C.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "forward_refs": [],
          "statement": "The coefficient recurrence in [[def-airy-initial-value-series]] makes both\nseries solve $y''=zy$. In a residue class $k\\equiv r\\pmod 3$, successive\nnonzero terms have absolute ratio\n\n$$\\frac{|z|^3}{(k+3)(k+2)}.$$\n\nIf the ratio of each term after a chosen first omitted term is bounded by\n$q<1$, that class's tail is at most the first omitted term's absolute value\ndivided by $1-q$. The series and their first two derivative series converge\nlocally uniformly on $\\mathbb C$."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-erf-series-geometric-tail",
          "deps": [
            "def-fresnel-erf-and-hypergeometric-series",
            "def-complex-exponential",
            "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere",
            "thm-termwise-differentiation-of-complex-power-series",
            "cor-complex-differentiability-implies-continuity",
            "thm-ftc-second-part",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "def-complex-line-integral-over-a-rectifiable-path",
            "thm-riemann-stieltjes-and-parametric-contour-integrals-agree"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Error-function series geometric tail bound",
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "DLMF, §7.6(i), equation 7.6.1",
                "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/7.6"
              },
              {
                "title": "N. M. Temme, “Numerical aspects of special functions,” Acta Numerica 17 (2008), §2",
                "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/11612/11612C.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "forward_refs": [],
          "statement": "For the $k$th term $e_k$ of the $\\operatorname{erf}$ series in\n[[def-fresnel-erf-and-hypergeometric-series]],\n\n$$\\frac{|e_{k+1}|}{|e_k|} =\\frac{|z|^2}{k+1}\\frac{2k+1}{2k+3} \\le\\frac{|z|^2}{k+1}.$$\n\nThe displayed quotient is asserted when $e_k\\ne0$. At $z=0$, every $e_k=0$, so the series and every tail are zero; the corresponding division-free inequality $|e_{k+1}|\\le |z|^2|e_k|/(k+1)$ holds there as well.\n\nThus, if $|z|^2/(K+2)=q<1$, the tail after terms $0,\\ldots,K$ is bounded by\n$|e_{K+1}|/(1-q)$. The series converges locally uniformly. For every\npiecewise-$C^1$ path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to\\mathbb C$ with $\\gamma(0)=0$ and\n$\\gamma(1)=z$, it equals\n$$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_\\gamma e^{-t^2}\\,dt,$$\nwhere the complex line integral is defined by\n[[def-complex-line-integral-over-a-rectifiable-path]]. In particular, the\nstraight-segment integral has this value, and the integral is path-independent."
        },
        {
          "id": "lem-fresnel-series-geometric-tail",
          "deps": [
            "def-fresnel-erf-and-hypergeometric-series",
            "def-complex-trigonometric-and-hyperbolic-functions",
            "thm-complex-trigonometric-and-hyperbolic-power-series",
            "thm-termwise-differentiation-of-complex-power-series",
            "cor-complex-differentiability-implies-continuity",
            "thm-ftc-second-part",
            "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
            "def-complex-line-integral-over-a-rectifiable-path",
            "thm-riemann-stieltjes-and-parametric-contour-integrals-agree"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Fresnel series geometric tail bounds",
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "DLMF, §7.6(i), equations 7.6.4 and 7.6.6",
                "url": "https://dlmf.nist.gov/7.6"
              },
              {
                "title": "N. M. Temme, “Numerical aspects of special functions,” Acta Numerica 17 (2008), §2",
                "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/11612/11612C.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "forward_refs": [],
          "statement": "For the terms $c_k$ of $C$ and $s_k$ of $S$ in\n[[def-fresnel-erf-and-hypergeometric-series]],\n\n$$\\frac{|c_{k+1}|}{|c_k|}\\le \\frac{\\pi^2|z|^4/4}{(2k+1)(2k+2)}, \\qquad \\frac{|s_{k+1}|}{|s_k|}\\le \\frac{\\pi^2|z|^4/4}{(2k+2)(2k+3)}.$$\n\nEach displayed quotient is asserted only when its denominator is nonzero. At $z=0$, every $c_k=s_k=0$, so both series and every tail are zero. The corresponding division-free inequalities obtained by multiplying by $|c_k|$ or $|s_k|$ hold for every $z$.\n\nOnce the applicable bound is at most $q<1$ for all terms after a first\nomitted term, the absolute tail is bounded by that term divided by $1-q$.\nBoth series converge locally uniformly on $\\mathbb C$. For every\npiecewise-$C^1$ path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to\\mathbb C$ with $\\gamma(0)=0$ and\n$\\gamma(1)=z$, they equal\n$$C(z)=\\int_\\gamma\\cos(\\pi t^2/2)\\,dt,\\qquad S(z)=\\int_\\gamma\\sin(\\pi t^2/2)\\,dt,$$\nwhere the complex line integral is defined by\n[[def-complex-line-integral-over-a-rectifiable-path]]. In particular, these\nintegrals are path-independent and agree with the straight-segment integrals."
        },
        {
