      "description": "{\"scope\": \"genuinely-new stable special-function algorithms\\n\\nA: `stable-special-function-recurrences-and-certified-evaluation`.\\n\\nDo not reauthor gamma/Bessel definitions. Reuse existing `def-euler-gamma-function`, `thm-stirling-formula-gamma`, `thm-gamma-functional-equation` only after exact statement inspection. Define finite-budget domain-partitioned evaluators for exp-scaled Bessel/modified Bessel and Airy, Fresnel sine/cosine integrals, gamma/log-gamma, erf/erfc, and a representative hypergeometric function. Each branch declares real/complex domain and branch cut, series/quadrature/continued fraction/asymptotic formula, scaling convention, remainder certificate, absolute/relative tolerance, and failure branch. No relative-error promise at zeros or cancellation without a conditioning factor.\\n\\nEssential substantial new methods: forward/backward three-term recurrence; Miller backward recurrence with a normalization and **explicit minimal/dominant separation hypotheses**; derive terminal-data contamination coefficient and finite-N truncation bound from two fundamental recurrence solutions and their discrete Wronskian. A stable direction is theorem/domain specific. Continued fractions use a proved convergent/remainder bound for the chosen coefficients; divergent asymptotic series use finite truncation with an independently proved remainder, not convergence. Include interval-enclosed power-series/geometric-tail evaluator and scaled asymptotic branch; show representation identities and overlap consistency on declared ranges. Prove recurrence/roundoff error amplification by inhomogeneous fundamental solutions. Algorithms may return inconclusive when the derived bound is too weak.\\n\\nSame-frontier PC-SFUN suppliers: representative Bessel/Airy/hypergeometric integral/series/ODE identities absent from inspected canonical interfaces, Wronskian/minimal-solution separation, certified tails, connection/branch formulas actually consumed. This is a full proof workload; citing DLMF is not a local proof. B: forward recurrence loses small J_n; backward recurrence plus normalization; erfc cancellation from 1-erf; overflow avoided by scaled I_n; relative error near an Airy zero; asymptotic optimal truncation; complex branch-cut mismatch. Sources: NIST DLMF computation chapters https://dlmf.nist.gov/3 , https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.74 , https://dlmf.nist.gov/9.17 , https://dlmf.nist.gov/8.25 ; Temme Numerical Methods for Special Functions https://doi.org/10.1002/9781118833050 . Reading pending.\", \"proof_route\": [\"Essential substantial new methods: forward/backward three-term recurrence; Miller backward recurrence with a normalization and **explicit minimal/dominant separation hypotheses**; derive terminal-data contamination coefficient and finite-N truncation bound from two fundamental recurrence solutions and their discrete Wronskian. A stable direction is theorem/domain specific. Continued fractions use a proved convergent/remainder bound for the chosen coefficients; divergent asymptotic series use finite truncation with an independently proved remainder, not convergence. Include interval-enclosed power-series/geometric-tail evaluator and scaled asymptotic branch; show representation identities and overlap consistency on declared ranges. Prove recurrence/roundoff error amplification by inhomogeneous fundamental solutions. Algorithms may return inconclusive when the derived bound is too weak.\", \"Same-frontier PC-SFUN suppliers: representative Bessel/Airy/hypergeometric integral/series/ODE identities absent from inspected canonical interfaces, Wronskian/minimal-solution separation, certified tails, connection/branch formulas actually consumed. This is a full proof workload; citing DLMF is not a local proof. B: forward recurrence loses small J_n; backward recurrence plus normalization; erfc cancellation from 1-erf; overflow avoided by scaled I_n; relative error near an Airy zero; asymptotic optimal truncation; complex branch-cut mismatch. Sources: NIST DLMF computation chapters https://dlmf.nist.gov/3 , https://dlmf.nist.gov/10.74 , https://dlmf.nist.gov/9.17 , https://dlmf.nist.gov/8.25 ; Temme Numerical Methods for Special Functions https://doi.org/10.1002/9781118833050 . Reading pending.\"], \"examples\": \"forward recurrence loses small J_n; backward recurrence plus normalization; erfc cancellation from 1-erf; overflow avoided by scaled I_n; relative error near an Airy zero; asymptotic optimal truncation; complex branch-cut mismatch.\"}",
