    "statement": "For $0<T\\le\\tfrac14$, the upward formula for the first two Boys orders is\n$$F_1(T)=\\frac{F_0(T)-e^{-T}}{2T}.$$\nBoth subtracted quantities are close to one, while their difference is of order $T$. If the input intervals for them have radii $r_0,r_e$, the exact upward affine image has radius $(r_0+r_e)/(2T)$. If its endpoints are converted outward once with finite errors $\\delta_-,\\delta_+$, the stored radius is $(r_0+r_e)/(2T)+(\\delta_-+\\delta_+)/2$. Rounding at intermediate nodes requires propagating their contributions as well. Thus fixed input radii can be strongly amplified as $T\\downarrow0$. The direct Taylor branch has the certified remainder $T^2/10$ for $F_0$ after its linear term, so it avoids this division by a small $T$."
