      "lem-linearly-degenerate-characteristic-contact",
      "def-mixed-characteristic-wave-curve",
      "lem-mixed-wave-curves-are-c1",
      "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 6,
    "statement": "Let $f:G\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be $C^3$, let $\\bar u\\in G$, and suppose on an open\nneighborhood $N$ of $\\bar u$ the system is strictly hyperbolic and each\ncharacteristic field is either genuinely nonlinear or linearly degenerate. Then\nthere are $\\delta,\\eta>0$ such that, whenever\n$\\|u_L-\\bar u\\|_2<\\delta$ and $\\|u_R-\\bar u\\|_2<\\delta$, there is a unique\nstrength vector $\\sigma\\in(-\\eta,\\eta)^m$ satisfying\n$$u_R=\\Psi_m(\\sigma_m;\\Psi_{m-1}(\\sigma_{m-1};\\cdots\\Psi_1(\\sigma_1;u_L)\\cdots)).$$\nFor the Riemann initial datum $u_0(x)=u_L$ for $x<0$ and $u_R$ for $x>0$,\nthe corresponding ordered superposition, with waves in increasing family\norder, is a self-similar weak solution, meaning it has the form\n$u(t,x)=q(x/t)$ for $t>0$. Its nonzero\ngenuinely nonlinear waves are rarefactions when $\\sigma_iD\\lambda_i r_i>0$\nand strict Lax shocks when $\\sigma_iD\\lambda_i r_i<0$; its linearly-degenerate\nwaves are contacts. The neighborhoods are local to $f,N,\\bar u$; no arbitrary\nlarge-data solution or uniqueness among all weak solutions is asserted.",
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "literature-derived",
      "proof": "ai-generated"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Alberto Bressan, Hyperbolic Conservation Laws: An Illustrated Tutorial",
          "url": "https://yulia-petrova.github.io/teaching/shocks/Bressan-tutorial.pdf",
          "locator": "§§3.3–3.4, printed pp. 24–29: elementary waves and the local wave-composition construction"
