          "title": "Randall J. LeVeque, Numerical Methods for Conservation Laws",
          "url": "https://tevza.org/home/course/modelling-II_2016/books/Leveque%20-%20Numerical%20Methods%20for%20Conservation%20Laws.pdf",
          "locator": "Chs. 7–8, §§7.6, 8.1–8.3, printed pp. 79–87: local system Riemann problems and integral curves"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "def-godunov-flux-from-a-local-riemann-solver",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Godunov flux from the local Riemann solution",
    "deps": [
      "thm-local-small-data-riemann-solver",
      "def-rankine-hugoniot-and-lax-shocks"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 7,
    "statement": "For left/right states in the neighborhood of\n[[thm-local-small-data-riemann-solver]], let $u(\\xi)$ be its self-similar\nprofile, $\\xi=x/t$. If the profile is continuous at $\\xi=0$, define the\n**Godunov flux** by $G(u_L,u_R)=f(u(0))$. If it has a jump there, let\n$u(0-)$ and $u(0+)$ be its one-sided traces and define\n$$G(u_L,u_R)=f(u(0-))=f(u(0+)).$$\nThese fluxes agree by Rankine–Hugoniot at speed zero, so the definition does not\ndepend on the arbitrary point value assigned at the jump. This is a local\ndefinition on the data neighborhood where the local solver is established.",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
