      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define admissible orthogonal meshes/transmissibilities for two-point isotropic diffusion, including boundary faces and discontinuous positive coefficients with harmonic face treatment. Prove local conservation, symmetric coercive matrix, discrete Poincare, maximum principle and convergence in a stated reconstructed energy norm. For anisotropic/nonorthogonal meshes define an explicit MPFA/mimetic method and prove its own coercivity/consistency under actual local geometric conditions; TPFA consistency is not claimed universally. Nonlinear monotone diffusion consumes PE12 compactness and identifies the graph under its exact assumptions.\", \"proof_route\": \"Flux cancellation gives discrete Green and energy; paths/translation bounds give meshuniform Poincare and compactness (prove geometric mesh conditions actually used); piecewise affine test recovery identifies weak gradients. Consistency errors vanish by orthogonality and regularity; nonlinear limit via Minty. Monotone matrix inverse gives positivity only when off-diagonal signs/irreducibility and boundary anchor are verified.\", \"examples\": \"orthogonal Voronoi diffusion, nonorthogonal TPFA inconsistency, anisotropic MPFA, heterogeneous interface flux.\"}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-fv-admissible-cell-face-mesh",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite-volume cell-face meshes and geometric regularity",
          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$ and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be a nonempty bounded connected open polyhedral set, meaning the interior of a finite union of bounded closed convex polytopes. A closed convex polytope is a bounded intersection of finitely many closed half-spaces with nonempty interior. A **cell-face mesh** $\\mathcal T=(\\mathcal K,\\mathcal F)$ consists of finitely many nonempty cells $K=\\operatorname{int}P_K\\subset\\Omega$, where each $P_K$ is a closed convex polytope, with pairwise disjoint interiors and $\\overline\\Omega=\\bigcup_{K\\in\\mathcal K}\\overline K$, together with the relative interiors of the $(d-1)$-dimensional faces of their closures. Each interior face $F\\in\\mathcal F_{\\rm int}$ is incident to exactly two cells $K,L$ and is denoted $F=K|L$; each boundary face $F\\in\\mathcal F_{\\rm bdy}$ is incident to one cell and is contained in $\\partial\\Omega$. In dimension one a face is an endpoint and has measure $|F|=1$.\n\nWrite $|K|$ for the $d$-dimensional volume of $K$, $|F|$ for the $(d-1)$-dimensional measure of $F$, $n_{K,F}$ for the unit normal outward from $K$, $x_K$ for a chosen point in $K$, and $x_F$ for the centroid of $F$. For every incident pair set $d_{K,F}=\\operatorname{dist}(x_K,F)>0$. The **cell adjacency graph** has one vertex for each cell and an edge for each interior face. A mesh is **boundary anchored** if every cell has a finite path in this graph to a cell incident to a boundary face; such a path can be chosen simple, with no repeated cells.\n\nFor a family $(\\mathcal T_h)$, put $h=\\max_{K\\in\\mathcal K}\\operatorname{diam}K$. The family is **uniformly regular** if there are fixed constants $c,C>0$ and $N_0\\in\\mathbb N$ such that, for every mesh and each of its cells and faces,\n$$ c h^d\\le |K|\\le C h^d,\\qquad c h^{d-1}\\le |F|\\le C h^{d-1},\\qquad c h\\le d_{K,F}\\le C h, $$\nand each cell has at most $N_0$ faces. A family is **refining** if $h\\to0$. These bounds are invoked only where explicitly assumed; a cell-face mesh alone is not asserted to be uniformly regular or orthogonal.\n\nThe finite-volume convention is that a face flux $Q_{K,F}$ is signed outward from $K$. If $F=K|L$ is an interior face, local conservation requires the single shared-flux identity $Q_{K,F}+Q_{L,F}=0$. The mesh drawing below labels the center-to-center vector and the outward normal; no orthogonality is part of this general mesh definition.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]\n  \\draw[thick] (0,0) -- (2,0) -- (2,2) -- (0,2) -- cycle;\n  \\draw[thick] (2,0) -- (4.3,0.5) -- (4.3,2.5) -- (2,2) -- cycle;\n  \\draw[thick] (2,0) -- (2,2);\n  \\node at (1,1) {$K$};\n  \\node at (3.15,1.25) {$L$};\n  \\node at (2,1.45) {$F=K|L$};\n  \\fill (1,1) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$x_K$};\n  \\fill (3.15,1.25) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$x_L$};\n  \\draw[->,blue,thick] (1,1) -- (3.15,1.25) node[midway,below] {$x_L-x_K$};\n  \\draw[->,red,thick] (2,1) -- (2.8,1) node[right] {$n_{K,F}$};\n  \\draw[->,red,thick] (2,1.8) -- (1.2,1.8) node[left] {$n_{L,F}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```\n\nThe cell-face mesh definition and the diagram fix only the objects, incidences, normals, and orientation. A two-point flux approximation adds a separate orthogonality condition; the hybrid mimetic method is formulated on the general cell-face geometry specified here.",
          "deps": [],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "strategy": "Specify cell, face, boundary, center, measure, adjacency, and mesh-family conventions, including the exact regularity bounds used later.",
          "axiom_base": "No choice axiom is used.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
