    "statement": "Let $\\mathcal T$ be a finite boundary-anchored TPFA mesh and let $T_e>0$ be its interior and boundary transmissibilities. A **boundary path family** assigns to each cell $K$ a finite simple path $\\gamma_K$ of adjacent interior faces, followed by one boundary face of its terminal cell; simple means it visits no cell more than once. Set $\\Delta_eu=u_K-u_L$ on an interior face $e=K|L$ with a fixed orientation, and set $\\Delta_eu=u_K$ on a boundary face of $K$. The sign of an interior increment is reversed when the path traverses the fixed orientation backward. Put\n$$ S_K=\\sum_{e\\in\\gamma_K}T_e^{-1},\\qquad C_{\\rm path}=\\max_{e}\\sum_{K:e\\in\\gamma_K}|K|S_K. $$\nThen every cell vector $u$ satisfies the discrete Poincaré inequality\n$$ \\sum_{K\\in\\mathcal K}|K|u_K^2\\le C_{\\rm path}\\sum_{e}T_e(\\Delta_eu)^2=C_{\\rm path}\\|u\\|_{E_h}^2. $$\nConsequently, any uniformly regular mesh family supplied with boundary path families whose $C_{\\rm path}$ are uniformly bounded has a mesh-uniform Poincaré constant. This theorem does not assert that every orthogonal mesh family has such a uniform path bound.",
