          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "statement": "Fix a bounded, connected polygonal open set $Ω\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ with a nonempty Dirichlet boundary segment. A finite **subdomain partition** is a family of connected polygonal open sets $Ω_i$, $1\\le i\\le N$, whose closures cover $\\overline\\Omega$, whose interiors are disjoint, and whose intersections are whole common sides, common vertices, or empty. The outer Dirichlet segment is a union of mesh boundary sides. The edge-adjacency graph of each triangulated subdomain is connected, and the subdomain adjacency graph is connected to the Dirichlet boundary. Each subdomain has a matching affine simplicial triangulation; the triangulations match on every shared side. The family has a fixed shape-regularity bound, quasiuniform mesh size $h_i$, uniformly bounded polygon side count, corner angles bounded below, inradius comparable to $H_i$, and a uniform interior-cone parameter. Write $H_i=\\operatorname{diam}(\\Omega_i)$ and $H/h=\\max_i H_i/h_i$.\n\nUse continuous piecewise-affine ($P_1$) scalar functions on each triangulation. The reference-element and face-matching conventions are those of [[def-reference-finite-element-and-unisolvent-dofs]], [[def-matching-affine-simplicial-meshes]], and [[def-broken-and-conforming-polynomial-spaces]]: local polynomials are affine, their nodal values determine them, and conforming functions have equal traces across a shared side. For each subdomain choose a coefficient $\\rho_i>0$, constant on $\\Omega_i$ but allowed to jump across interfaces. Its local energy is\n\n$$a_i(v,w)=\\rho_i\\sum_{K\\subset\\Omega_i}\\int_K\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla w\\,dx.$$\n\nThe global finite-element space consists of the continuous piecewise-affine functions on the subdomain meshes that vanish on the outer Dirichlet boundary, with energy $a(v,w)=\\sum_i a_i(v|_{\\Omega_i},w|_{\\Omega_i})$. Local interior degrees of freedom are the mesh vertices strictly inside $\\Omega_i$; local boundary degrees of freedom are its remaining vertices, with outer Dirichlet values fixed to zero. A **floating local block** means the same local energy before any outer-boundary values are fixed. A **subdomain corner** is a vertex of its polygonal boundary.\n\nAll constants in later two-dimensional estimates may depend on the fixed polygon-shape, side-count, and shape-regularity bounds, but not on $h$, $H$, the number of subdomains, or the positive numbers $\\rho_i$.",
