          "statement": "Fix an integer $N\\ge1$, put $h=1/N$, and let $x_j=jh$ for $j=0,\\ldots,N$. The cells are $K_j=[x_{j-1},x_j]$. For $g_0,g_1\\in\\mathbb R$, let $S_h(g_0,g_1)$ be the finite-dimensional space of continuous functions on $[0,1]$ that are affine on each cell and have endpoint values $g_0,g_1$. Write $V_h=S_h(0,0)$ for the homogeneous test and trial space. “P1” means polynomial degree at most one on each interval.\n\nLet $\\epsilon>0$, $b\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and $f\\in C([0,1])$. The stationary boundary-value problem is to find $u\\in C^2([0,1])$ such that $-\\epsilon u''+b u'=f$ on $(0,1)$, with prescribed endpoint values $u(0)=g_0$ and $u(1)=g_1$. Its dimensionless cell Péclet number is $\\mathrm{Pe}=|b|h/(2\\epsilon)>0$. The stability theorem on this page uses the homogeneous branch $g_0=g_1=0$; the general endpoint notation also permits the boundary-layer example.",
