    "title": "The H1 trace and lifting theorem on a compact Lipschitz boundary",
    "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and let $\\Gamma$ be any compact Lipschitz boundary in the geometry of [[def-bem-lipschitz-polyhedral-boundary-and-trace-spaces]]; no polyhedral restriction is imposed. The interior trace $\\gamma^-:H^1(\\Omega^-)\\to H^{1/2}(\\Gamma)$ is bounded. On every bounded exterior boundary collar $C=\\Omega^+\\cap U$, where $U$ is a bounded neighborhood of $\\Gamma$ with Lipschitz collar boundary, the exterior trace $\\gamma^+:H^1(C)\\to H^{1/2}(\\Gamma)$ is bounded. The corresponding localized assertion holds over each boundary chart. Thus an exterior function has this trace whenever it is $H^1$ up to the boundary on such collars; membership in $H^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega^+)$ alone does not assert a boundary trace. There is a bounded linear right inverse $L:H^{1/2}(\\Gamma)\\to H^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\gamma(Lg)=g$. There is also a bounded extension operator $E:H^1(\\Omega^-)\\to H^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ with $(Eu)|_{\\Omega^-}=u$.\n\nLet $\\dot H^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ denote the completion of $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ in the norm $\\|u\\|_{\\dot H^1}=\\|\\nabla u\\|_{L^2}$. Its elements have an $L^6$ representative, the trace $\\gamma:\\dot H^1(\\mathbb R^3)\\to H^{1/2}(\\Gamma)$ is bounded, and the right inverse above may be chosen with compact support near $\\Gamma$.",
