    "id": "def-bem-laplace-layer-potentials-and-energy-spaces",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Weak Laplace single-layer potentials and energy spaces",
    "statement": "Write $G_3$ for the Newton kernel of [[def-bem-newtonian-kernel-in-three-dimensions]]. For a smooth density $\\lambda$ on $\\Gamma$, the classical single-layer potential is\n$$S\\lambda(x)=\\int_\\Gamma G_3(x,y)\\lambda(y)\\,dS_y,\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\Gamma.$$\n\nLet $\\dot H^1(\\mathbb R^3)$ be the completion of $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$ in the norm $\\|u\\|_{\\dot H^1}=\\|\\nabla u\\|_{L^2(\\mathbb R^3)}$, using its $L^6$ representative and the trace from [[lem-bem-lipschitz-trace-and-lifting]]. For $\\lambda\\in H^{-1/2}(\\Gamma)$, consider the equation for $u\\in\\dot H^1(\\mathbb R^3)$\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\nabla u\\cdot\\nabla v\\,dx=\\langle\\lambda,\\gamma v\\rangle_\\Gamma\\qquad(v\\in\\dot H^1(\\mathbb R^3)).$$\nCall such a function a weak single-layer potential and write $S\\lambda=u$ when it exists. Define the exterior energy space $E^+(\\Omega^+)=\\{u\\in H^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega^+):u\\in L^6(\\Omega^+),\\ \\nabla u\\in L^2(\\Omega^+)\\}$. For a weakly harmonic function $u^-\\in H^1(\\Omega^-)$, define its weak outward conormal by $\\langle\\partial_n^-u^-,g\\rangle=\\int_{\\Omega^-}\\nabla u^-\\cdot\\nabla L^-g$, where $L^-g$ is any $H^1$ trace lift. For a harmonic $u^+\\in E^+(\\Omega^+)$, use the same fixed normal $n$ by $\\langle\\partial_n^+u^+,g\\rangle=-\\int_{\\Omega^+}\\nabla u^+\\cdot\\nabla L^+g$ for a compactly supported local lift. In either case the value is independent of the lift: the difference of two lifts has zero trace, and weak harmonicity makes its gradient pairing zero. The exterior formula also gives $\\int_{\\Omega^+}\\nabla u^+\\cdot\\nabla v^+=-\\langle\\partial_n^+u^+,\\gamma^+v^+\\rangle$ for every $v^+\\in E^+(\\Omega^+)$. Indeed, subtract a compactly supported lift of $\\gamma^+v^+$; the remaining zero-trace finite-energy function is approximated in gradient norm by compactly supported zero-trace functions using radial cutoffs: $\\|v^+\\nabla\\chi_R\\|_2\\le C\\|v^+\\|_{L^6(\\{R<|x|<2R\\})}\\to0$. Boundary-chart flattening, zero extension, inward translation, and convolution approximate each zero-trace cutoff by interior smooth tests, so weak harmonicity makes its gradient pairing zero. This proves the energy identity without a flux assumption at infinity. Existence, uniqueness, agreement with the displayed kernel for smooth densities, and the conormal jump are proved in [[thm-bem-single-layer-transmission-mapping-and-coercivity]]. The weak definition fixes the normalization at infinity through $u\\in L^6(\\mathbb R^3)$; arbitrary additive constants are excluded.",
