      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define first-order-system least-squares Poisson on H(div)×H1_0 with residual `(sigma+grad u, div sigma-f)` and the correct norm. Prove residual norm equivalent to H(div)×H1_0 error and Galerkin best approximation; boundary conditions are encoded rather than omitted. Define ultraweak Poisson DPG on a stated simplicial mesh with skeleton H1/2 and H-1/2 quotient trace spaces, optimal trial-to-test map via the existing Riesz theorem, and a practical enriched broken test space. Prove continuous inf-sup, ideal energy best approximation, practical quasi-optimality and residual estimator equivalence using a concrete uniformly bounded Fortin operator for the chosen polynomial family/enrichment degree. State the exact degree enrichment from its proved construction. An arbitrary finite test space receives no stability claim.\", \"proof_route\": \"For least-squares recover grad u and sigma from the residual using weak Poisson energy, integration by parts and Poincare; triangle/divergence bounds give both inequalities. For ultraweak form define skeleton norms as minimum-energy extension norms, prove trace surjectivity and extension/lifting without consuming a C1-only boundary theorem on simplices. Prove injectivity/adjoint graph stability and BNB (NA34), then Riesz maps residual to optimal tests. Construct the practical Fortin map locally by moment constraints/bubble corrections, prove polynomial solvability/scaled norm bounds and face trace preservation, then transfer inf-sup. Computed QR/iterative algebraic residual and quadrature perturbations are separately bounded; normal equations still square the matrix condition number under the canonical theorem.\", \"examples\": \"residual certificate for Poisson, omitted boundary residual nonuniqueness, too-small test space, ideal/practical test comparison.\"}",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "lem-na-ultraweak-poisson-rotation-trace-2d",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "title": "Planar rotation carries H(div) normal traces to tangential traces",
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. For a nondegenerate oriented triangle $K\\subset\\mathbb R^2$, set $R(a,b)=(-b,a)$, let $n$ be cell-outward and $t=Rn$. If $\\sigma\\in H(\\operatorname{div};K)$ is real, then $R\\sigma\\in H(\\operatorname{curl};K)$, $\\operatorname{curl}(R\\sigma)=\\operatorname{div}\\sigma$ distributionally, and $\\int_K(R\\sigma\\cdot R\\nabla\\phi+\\operatorname{curl}(R\\sigma)\\phi)=\\langle\\gamma_n\\sigma,[\\phi]\\rangle$ for all $\\phi\\in H^1(K)$. Thus weak tangential trace equals weak normal trace under $t=Rn$.\n\nHere $H(\\operatorname{curl};K):=\\{w\\in L^2(K;\\mathbb R^2):\\partial_1w_2-\\partial_2w_1\\in L^2(K)\\}$ with its graph norm. The weak tangential trace is the functional on $H^1(K)/H^1_0(K)$ defined by the integral in the Statement; the computation below proves it is the rotation of the PE08 weak normal trace.",
          "deps": [
            "def-na-hdiv-space",
            "lem-na-hdiv-weak-normal-trace",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "strategy": "Define the two-dimensional rotated H(curl) interface locally from the actual H(div) trace supplier; verify the distributional derivative identity and orientation sign without importing the 3D PE09 trace Statement.",
          "justified_by": [],
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jay Gopalakrishnan and Weifeng Qiu, An analysis of the practical DPG method",
                "url": "https://wiki.math.ntnu.no/_media/tma4183/2017v/1107.4293.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 15-page paper; §2 Theorem 2.1 and proof; §§3.1–3.4, including Lemmas 3.1–3.3 on ultraweak Poisson and Fortin maps."
              },
              {
                "title": "Leszek Demkowicz and Jay Gopalakrishnan, Analysis of the DPG method for the Poisson equation",
                "url": "https://web.pdx.edu/~gjay/pub/DPGlaplace.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 22-page paper; §§3.1–3.2, Lemmas 4.1–4.4, Theorem 4.2. The graph inf-sup proof assumes simply connected Lipschitz domain."
              },
              {
                "title": "Leszek Demkowicz, Mathematical Theory of Finite Elements",
                "url": "https://users.ices.utexas.edu/~leszek/classes/EM394H/book.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 229-page course monograph; §§5.1–5.2 ideal/practical DPG; §§5.6.2 and 5.6.4 Fortin and mixed stability."
              },
              {
                "title": "C. Carstensen, L. Demkowicz, and J. Gopalakrishnan, A Posteriori Error Control for DPG Methods",
                "url": "https://web.pdx.edu/~gjay/pub/cdg.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 21-page paper; full §2 and Theorem 2.1 proof, §3.1. The Poisson stability application cites the same simply-connected source."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "def-na-least-squares-poisson-first-order",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "First-order least-squares formulation of the Dirichlet Poisson problem",
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ be bounded connected Lipschitz and $f\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$. Solve $-\\Delta u=f$, $u\\in H^1_0(\\Omega)$; set $\\sigma=-\\nabla u$, so $\\operatorname{div}\\sigma=f$. For a finite conforming shape-regular triangular mesh, define $X_h=RT_0(\\mathcal T_h)\\times(P_1(\\mathcal T_h)\\cap H^1_0(\\Omega))$ and $J_h(\\sigma_h,u_h)=\\|\\sigma_h+\\nabla u_h\\|_2^2+\\|\\operatorname{div}\\sigma_h-f\\|_2^2$. Here $RT_0(K)=P_0(K)^2+xP_0(K)$ and $P_1$ is continuous piecewise affine. The real Sobolev-space and zero-boundary conventions are [[def-sobolev-space-wkp-and-its-norm]] and [[def-wkp-zero-as-a-sobolev-closure]]; H(div), mesh, and RT conventions are [[def-na-hdiv-space]] and [[def-na-simplicial-mesh-rt-bdm-spaces]]. This is the plan-spec PE30 sign convention; negating the design flux converts its residuals to these and preserves the squared objective. Integrals are exact; geometry, quadrature and roundoff errors are excluded.",
