          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ be any bounded connected Lipschitz polygon, including polygons with holes, and let $\\mathcal T_h$ be any finite conforming shape-regular triangular mesh. Use the exact two-dimensional P1/R3 practical family selected in PE30: volume fields $\\sigma_h|_K\\in P_1(K)^2$ and $u_h|_K\\in P_1(K)$; compatible global scalar traces of degree at most two on facets, zero on boundary facets; conservative global normal traces of degree at most one; and broken tests $V_r=\\prod_K(P_3(K)^2\\times P_3(K))$, with the graph inner product inherited from $V$. The local Fortin projection $\\Pi:V\\to V_r$ is that of [[lem-na-practical-dpg-fortin-p1-r3]], and $C_\\Pi$ is its operator norm bound. For each $f\\in L^2(\\Omega)$, the continuous ultraweak solution $x$ and practical solution $x_h$ exist uniquely, with $$\\|x-x_h\\|_U\\le(1+5C_\\Pi C_*)\\inf_{z_h\\in U_h}\\|x-z_h\\|_U.$$ For every $\\widetilde x_h\\in U_h$, put $\\eta=\\|F_f-B\\widetilde x_h\\|_{V_r'}$, where $V_r'$ is the dual of $V_r$ with its induced norm, and $\\operatorname{osc}(F_f)=\\|F_f\\circ(I-\\Pi)\\|_{V'}$. Then $$C_*^{-2}\\|x-\\widetilde x_h\\|_U^2\\le\\eta^2+(C_\\Pi\\eta+\\operatorname{osc}(F_f))^2,\\qquad \\eta\\le5\\|x-\\widetilde x_h\\|_U,$$ and $$\\operatorname{osc}(F_f)\\le C_{\\rm osc}\\left(\\sum_Kh_K^2\\|f-f_K\\|_{L^2(K)}^2\\right)^{1/2},\\qquad f_K=|K|^{-1}\\int_Kf,$$ where $h_K=\\operatorname{diam}(K)$ and $C_{\\rm osc}$ is the cellwise constant from the Fortin map's mean-preserving local $L^2$ estimate. The constants $C_*$, $C_\\Pi$, and $C_{\\rm osc}$ are independent of mesh size for the fixed domain and shape-regular family. The practical corollary is two-dimensional; the continuous graph theorem also covers dimension three.",
