    "statement": "Fix $\\varepsilon>0$, grid width $h$, particle count $P\\ge1$, and $T>0$. Use uniform RK4 times $t_n=n\\Delta t$ for integers $n\\ge0$, with $0<\\Delta t\\le h_0$, where $h_0$ is the positive admitted step-width bound in the RK4 tube/defect hypotheses below. Retain all $t_n\\le T$; no integer value of $T/\\Delta t$ is required. Assume the smooth fixed-regularization hypotheses of [[thm-periodic-pic-smooth-density-grid-force-consistency]], and that the translated unit-source regularized densities $\\eta_\\varepsilon(\\cdot-y)-L^{-d}$ satisfy its derivative bounds uniformly in $y$. Let $\\delta_{\\varepsilon,h}$ be the uniform phase-kernel defect from [[thm-periodic-pic-mean-field-error-with-force-defect]]. The consistency theorem gives the explicit estimate\n$$ \\delta_{\\varepsilon,h} \\le |cQ|\\left[ L^{d/2}h^{2-d/2}\\bigl(\\gamma_{N,L}C_{\\rm src}^{(1)}+C_3^{(1)}\\bigr) +C_{\\rm int}^{(1)}h^2 \\right], $$ where $C_{\\rm src}^{(1)},C_3^{(1)},C_{\\rm int}^{(1)}$ are the constants of that theorem evaluated at a unit point source; translation of the continuum source leaves their derivative suprema unchanged, while the shape quadrature constants are independent of the translate. Define the RK4 empirical measure by $\\nu_{P,h}^{\\Delta t}(t_n)=P^{-1}\\sum_{p=1}^P\\delta_{z_p^{\\Delta t}(t_n)}$. Put $$ \\delta_0=W_1(\\nu_0^h,\\mu_0),\\qquad C_{\\rm RK}= \\begin{cases} C_4(e^{L_*T}-1)/L_*,&L_*>0,\\\\ C_4T,&L_*=0, \\end{cases} $$ using the constants of [[thm-periodic-pic-rk4-finite-time-error-on-a-controlled-tube]]. For exact initialization, a common admissible initial coupling, and the same compact-tube hypotheses, the RK4 empirical measure $\\nu_{P,h}^{\\Delta t}(t_n)$ and regularized characteristic solution $\\mu_\\varepsilon(t_n)$ satisfy $$ W_1\\!\\left(\\nu_{P,h}^{\\Delta t}(t_n),\\mu_\\varepsilon(t_n)\\right) \\le \\frac{C_{\\rm RK}}{\\sqrt P}\\Delta t^4 +e^{2L_\\varepsilon T}\\left(\\delta_0+T\\delta_{\\varepsilon,h}\\right), \\qquad t_n\\le T. $$\nThe terms are time error, prescribed initial empirical error, and fixed-$\\varepsilon$ mesh-force error. No universal rate for $\\delta_0$ and no $\\varepsilon\\to0$ singular Vlasov–Poisson limit is asserted."
  }
