      "companion": "reaction-diffusion-invariants-and-pattern-discretization-examples",
      "requires": [
        "parabolic-finite-difference-methods",
        "finite-volume-diffusion-and-mimetic-fluxes",
        "imex-and-operator-splitting-methods",
        "finite-generator-master-equations-and-uniformization"
      ],
      "description": "{\"scope\": \"Define semilinear vector reaction-diffusion on bounded/periodic domains, quasi-positive reaction, mass conservation/dissipation and invariant rectangles. Prove positivity/invariant-set preservation for a stated mass-lumped FEM or monotone FV diffusion plus a positive reaction step and actual dt bound; prove mass balance. For smooth bounded solutions prove a finite-time error estimate with reaction Lipschitz constants on a common invariant tube. Linearize about a homogeneous equilibrium and derive continuous versus discrete dispersion relations/Turing criterion using the actual discrete Laplacian spectrum. This is linear instability, not existence of nonlinear stable patterns. Crossdiffusion convergence needs a separately stated entropy/coercivity structure, not componentwise maximum principle.\", \"proof_route\": \"M-matrix inverse or convex explicit update, quasi-positive reaction boundary signs, discrete comparison; sum stoichiometric conservation vectors. The selected monotone finite-volume branch uses exact reaction-then-diffusion Lie composition. For smooth periodic solutions on rectangular centered grids, prove the spatial residual, reaction/diffusion defect and maximum-norm stability locally with constants independent of mesh width, and obtain the finite-time error O(tau+h_max^2). Transfer this estimate to rectangular cell-centered no-flux TPFA grids by even reflection under the stated C1-in-time/C4-in-space reflection compatibility. The broader admissible-mesh positivity and mass results do not assert accuracy on every admissible mesh family. NA36's conforming FE/Ritz spatial estimates apply only to a separately selected conforming FE branch satisfying their actual variational, boundary, regularity, projection and axiom hypotheses; they do not supply a TPFA or centered-difference estimate. Fourier/eigenmode diagonalization gives dispersion matrix J-lambda D and its trace/determinant criterion in two species.\", \"examples\": \"Fisher–KPP, activator/inhibitor two-species, finite-grid Turing shift, positivity failure of consistent mass FEM.\"}",
      "items": [
  {
    "id": "def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity",
    "kind": "definition",
    "deps": [],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "title": "Reaction vector fields and quasi-positivity",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Willem Hundsdorfer, Numerical Solution of Advection-Diffusion-Reaction Equations, lecture notes for Ph.D. course, 1996",
          "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/4797/4797D.pdf",
          "locator": "§0, printed p. 1: concentrations of several species and a coupled reaction vector; §4, printed pp. 26–27: the boundary sign criterion for positivity of a locally Lipschitz semidiscrete ODE."
        },
        {
          "title": "Luca Doria, Numerical Algorithms, lecture notes, Johannes Gutenberg-Universität Mainz, 2022",
          "url": "https://www.staff.uni-mainz.de/doria/NumAlg/NumAlg.pdf",
          "locator": "§§10.6–10.7, PDF pp. 149–151: scalar and vector reaction-diffusion formulations and the Jacobian linearization at a homogeneous equilibrium."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be locally Lipschitz. Write $F_i$ for its $i$th component. The autonomous reaction equation is $z'(t)=F(z(t))$. The field is **quasi-positive** on $U\\cap[0,\\infty)^m$ if, for every $i$ and every $z\\in U\\cap[0,\\infty)^m$ with $z_i=0$, one has $F_i(z)\\ge0$. This condition concerns only the coordinate faces of the nonnegative orthant.\n\nFor a rectangle $R=\\prod_{i=1}^m[a_i,b_i]\\subset U$ with $a_i<b_i$, the field is **inward-pointing on $R$** if $F_i(z)\\ge0$ whenever $z\\in R$ and $z_i=a_i$, and $F_i(z)\\le0$ whenever $z\\in R$ and $z_i=b_i$. If $a_i=0$ for every $i$, quasi-positivity supplies the lower-face half of this condition, but the upper-face inequalities remain separate hypotheses. Neither boundary condition by itself asserts that a numerical update preserves $R$.\n\nFor smoothness-dependent results below, $F\\in C^4(U,\\mathbb R^m)$ will be stated explicitly. No smoothness beyond local Lipschitz continuity is built into this definition."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-periodic-reaction-diffusion-grid",
    "kind": "definition",
    "deps": [
      "def-parabolic-theta-finite-difference-scheme",
      "lem-periodic-heat-difference-amplification-symbol"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "title": "Periodic rectangular reaction-diffusion grid",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Luca Doria, Numerical Algorithms, lecture notes, Johannes Gutenberg-Universität Mainz, 2022",
          "url": "https://www.staff.uni-mainz.de/doria/NumAlg/NumAlg.pdf",
          "locator": "§§10.6–10.8, printed pp. 144–148 (PDF pp. 149–153): multispecies reaction-diffusion equations, two-species linear stability and Turing context, and the FitzHugh–Nagumo illustration with a periodic centered-difference Laplacian (Listing 10.1)."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Fix $d\\ge1$, side lengths $L_j>0$, and integers $N_j\\ge3$. Put $h_j=L_j/N_j$, $\\mathbb T_L^d=\\prod_{j=1}^d(\\mathbb R/L_j\\mathbb Z)$, and $G_h=\\prod_{j=1}^d(\\mathbb Z/N_j\\mathbb Z)$. Grid nodes are $x_i=((i_j+\\tfrac12)h_j)_{j=1}^d$ modulo the side lengths, grid indices wrap modulo $N_j$, and $P_hu=(u(x_i))_{i\\in G_h}$ denotes sampling. The cell volume is $m_h=\\prod_jh_j$. The positive periodic heat stencil is the unit-diffusivity specialization of the periodic operator in [[def-parabolic-theta-finite-difference-scheme]]:\n$$\n(A_h^{\\rm per}v)_i=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\frac{2v_i-v_{i+e_j}-v_{i-e_j}}{h_j^2}.\n$$\nFor $s$ species with diffusivities $d_r\\ge0$, the reaction-diffusion grid generator acts on species $r$ by $-d_rA_h^{\\rm per}$. Each grid node has weight $m_h=\\prod_jh_j$; the stencil has zero row and column sums, so each species' weighted sum is conserved by diffusion. Its matrix representation satisfies $L_h=m_hA_h^{\\rm per}$, with symmetric $L_h$, nonpositive off-diagonal entries and $L_h\\mathbf1=0$.\n\nThe sign convention is that $A_h^{\\rm per}$ is nonnegative and $-d_rA_h^{\\rm per}$ generates diffusion. The exact eigenvalues of the positive stencil are supplied by [[lem-periodic-heat-difference-amplification-symbol]]. This grid is the only spatial discretization used in the periodic error theorem; no mesh-uniform claim for general finite-volume meshes is made there."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-reaction-invariant-rectangle",
    "kind": "definition",
    "deps": [
      "def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "title": "Inward reaction rectangles",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Willem Hundsdorfer, Numerical Solution of Advection-Diffusion-Reaction Equations, lecture notes for Ph.D. course, 1996",
          "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/4797/4797D.pdf",
          "locator": "§4, printed pp. 26–27: inward signs on the boundary of the nonnegative orthant for a positive ODE flow. A bounded rectangle additionally requires upper-face signs, stated here."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be locally Lipschitz, and let $R=\\prod_{i=1}^m[a_i,b_i]\\subset U$ with finite $a_i<b_i$. The rectangle is **reaction-inward** if, for every $i$, $F_i(z)\\ge0$ on the lower face $z_i=a_i$ and $F_i(z)\\le0$ on the upper face $z_i=b_i$, with all other coordinates in their intervals. It is **reaction-invariant** if the solution map $\\Phi_F^t$ is defined for all $t\\ge0$ on $R$ and $\\Phi_F^t(R)\\subseteq R$ for every $t\\ge0$.\n\nFor a rectangle with $a_i=0$ for all $i$, the lower-face condition is exactly quasi-positivity from [[def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity]]. The upper-face condition is independent and must be checked separately. The inward-sign condition is a hypothesis, not by definition an invariance theorem."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-stoichiometric-mass-invariant",
    "kind": "definition",
    "deps": [
      "def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 1,
    "title": "Stoichiometric conservation and dissipation functionals",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Willem Hundsdorfer, Numerical Solution of Advection-Diffusion-Reaction Equations, lecture notes for Ph.D. course, 1996",
          "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/4797/4797D.pdf",
          "locator": "§0, printed p. 1: mass conservation as a qualitative requirement for transport-reaction discretizations. The weighted linear-functional convention here is defined explicitly."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be the reaction vector field of [[def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity]], and let $R\\subset U$ be a state set. A vector $c\\in[0,\\infty)^m$ is a **stoichiometric conservation vector on $R$** if $c^TF(z)=0$ for every $z\\in R$. It is a **stoichiometric dissipation vector on $R$** if $c^TF(z)\\le0$ for every $z\\in R$. The corresponding pointwise mass is $c^Tz$.\n\nFor a measurable domain $\\Omega$, the continuous total mass is $\\mathcal M_c(u)=\\int_\\Omega c^Tu(x)\\,dx$. For any finite index set $I$ with assigned positive weights $m_i$, the discrete total mass of states $U_i\\in\\mathbb R^m$ is $\\mathcal M_{c,h}(U)=\\sum_{i\\in I}m_ic^TU_i$. The weights and index set are part of the discrete model. Conservation and dissipation always refer to these stated functionals and the stated state set; no claim is made for a different weight vector or for reactions outside $R$."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-semilinear-reaction-diffusion-system",
    "kind": "definition",
    "deps": [
      "def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity",
      "def-reaction-invariant-rectangle"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 2,
    "title": "Semilinear vector reaction-diffusion systems",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Willem Hundsdorfer, Numerical Solution of Advection-Diffusion-Reaction Equations, lecture notes for Ph.D. course, 1996",
          "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/4797/4797D.pdf",
          "locator": "§0, printed p. 1: the vector concentration equation for several species with diffusion and local reaction terms; §9, printed pp. 60–64: splitting the evolution into reaction and diffusion subproblems."
        },
        {
          "title": "Luca Doria, Numerical Algorithms, lecture notes, Johannes Gutenberg-Universität Mainz, 2022",
          "url": "https://www.staff.uni-mainz.de/doria/NumAlg/NumAlg.pdf",
          "locator": "§§10.6–10.7, PDF pp. 149–151: the vector reaction-diffusion equation and linearization at a homogeneous equilibrium."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $D=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\ldots,d_m)$ with $d_i\\ge0$, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ be as in [[def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity]]. A **semilinear vector reaction-diffusion system** is\n$$\nu_t=D\\Delta u+F(u),\\qquad u(x,0)=u_0(x),\n$$\non either a flat periodic torus $\\mathbb T_L^d$ or a bounded connected polyhedral domain $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with homogeneous no-flux conditions $\\partial_nu_i=0$ for every component. The diffusion matrix is diagonal and independent of $u$; this definition does not include cross-diffusion.\n\nA classical solution is **bounded in the rectangle $R$** on $[0,T]$ if $u(x,t)\\in R$ at every point and time in its stated solution class, with $R$ as in [[def-reaction-invariant-rectangle]]. This item fixes the continuous PDE convention; the finite-volume and periodic discrete operators are defined separately."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-fv-tpfa-no-flux-diffusion-specialization",
    "kind": "definition",
    "deps": [
      "def-fv-admissible-cell-face-mesh",
      "def-tpfa-harmonic-face-scheme"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 2,
    "title": "No-flux TPFA diffusion generator",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "R. Eymard, T. Gallouët, and R. Herbin, Finite Volume Methods, updated author text, 2019",
          "url": "https://raphaeleh.github.io/PUBLI/bookevol.pdf",
          "locator": "Chapter 9 §9.2, PDF pp. 38–44: interior-face transmissibilities and shared conservative TPFA fluxes."
        },
        {
          "title": "Jérôme Droniou, Finite volume schemes for diffusion equations: introduction to and review of modern methods, 2014",
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.1567",
          "locator": "§2, PDF pp. 8–10: orthogonal TPFA fluxes, positive transmissibilities, and the weighted stiffness matrix."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $\\mathcal T=(\\mathcal K,\\mathcal F)$ be a finite TPFA-admissible cell-face mesh from [[def-fv-admissible-cell-face-mesh]] and [[def-tpfa-harmonic-face-scheme]]. Impose homogeneous no-flux boundary conditions: boundary faces contribute zero flux and therefore no conductance. For adjacent cells $K,L$, set\n$$\nc_{KL}=\\sum_{F=K|L}T_F,\n$$\nwhere the sum is over their common interior faces and is zero when they are not adjacent. Set $c_{KL}=c_{LK}$ and $m_K=|K|$. If more than one face separates a cell pair, summing their conductances gives the same cell-balance flux as retaining the faces separately. Define the symmetric matrix $L_h$ by $(L_h)_{KK}=\\sum_{L\\ne K}c_{KL}$ and $(L_h)_{KL}=-c_{KL}$ for $K\\ne L$.\n\nFor species diffusivities $d_r\\ge0$, the no-flux finite-volume diffusion equation is $M U_r'+d_r L_h U_r=0$ for each species $r$, where $M=\\operatorname{diag}(|K|)$. Its component equation is\n$$\n|K|(U_{K,r})'=d_r\\sum_{L:c_{KL}>0}c_{KL}(U_{L,r}-U_{K,r}).\n$$\nThe equality is the usual shared-face TPFA balance with outward flux $T_F(U_{K,r}-U_{L,r})$ and no boundary flux. The matrix $L_h$ is symmetric, has zero row sums and nonpositive off-diagonal entries, and obeys $v^{\\mathsf T}L_hv=\\sum_{\\{K,L\\}}c_{KL}(v_K-v_L)^2\\ge0$, where the sum is over unordered adjacent cell pairs. With $M=\\operatorname{diag}(|K|)$, the generator $-M^{-1}L_h$ is self-adjoint in the weighted pairing $\\langle v,w\\rangle_M=v^{\\mathsf T}Mw$. This definition makes no accuracy claim for arbitrary admissible meshes."
  },
  {
    "id": "thm-quasipositive-reaction-flow-preserves-invariant-rectangle-and-mass",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "deps": [
      "def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity",
      "def-reaction-invariant-rectangle",
      "def-stoichiometric-mass-invariant",
      "thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness",
      "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution",
      "thm-gronwall-integral-inequality",
      "thm-ftc-second-part"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 2,
    "title": "Inward reaction fields preserve rectangles and stoichiometric mass",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Willem Hundsdorfer, Numerical Solution of Advection-Diffusion-Reaction Equations, lecture notes for Ph.D. course, 1996",
          "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/4797/4797D.pdf",
          "locator": "§4, printed pp. 26–27: inward boundary signs for positive semidiscrete reaction ODEs; the local proof supplies the full rectangle argument."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $R=\\prod_{i=1}^m[a_i,b_i]$ be the reaction-inward rectangle of [[def-reaction-invariant-rectangle]]. Assume $F$ from [[def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity]] is continuously differentiable on an open neighborhood of $R$, and its derivative is bounded on some closed neighborhood of $R$. For any $z_0\\in R$, the IVP $z'=F(z)$ has a unique solution for every $t\\ge0$, and $z(t)\\in R$. If $c$ is a conservation vector on $R$, then $c^Tz(t)=c^Tz_0$; if it is a dissipation vector, then $c^Tz(t)$ is nonincreasing. When every $a_i=0$, quasi-positivity implies the lower-face condition on $R$; upper-face inwardness remains a separate hypothesis."
  },
  {
    "id": "lem-forward-euler-reaction-step-preserves-invariant-rectangle-and-mass",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "deps": [
      "def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity",
      "def-reaction-invariant-rectangle",
      "def-stoichiometric-mass-invariant"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 2,
    "title": "A bounded forward-Euler reaction step preserves an inward rectangle",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Willem Hundsdorfer, Numerical Solution of Advection-Diffusion-Reaction Equations, lecture notes for Ph.D. course, 1996",
          "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/4797/4797D.pdf",
          "locator": "§6, printed pp. 39–40: forward-Euler positivity under an explicit reaction Lipschitz step bound; the local proof gives the rectangle and mass version."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $R=\\prod_{i=1}^m[a_i,b_i]$ be reaction-inward for $F$ from [[def-reaction-vector-field-and-quasipositivity]]. Assume for each $i$ there is a coordinate Lipschitz constant $L_i\\ge0$ such that $|F_i(z)-F_i(w)|\\le L_i|z_i-w_i|$ whenever $z,w\\in R$ differ only in coordinate $i$. Define\n$$\n\\tau_R=\\min_{i:L_i>0}L_i^{-1},\n$$\nwith $\\tau_R=+\\infty$ if every $L_i=0$. For $z\\in R$ and $0\\le\\tau\\le\\tau_R$, the forward-Euler reaction step $z^+=z+\\tau F(z)$ lies in $R$. For every conservation vector $c$ on $R$, $c^Tz^+=c^Tz$; for every dissipation vector, $c^Tz^+\\le c^Tz$."
  },
  {
    "id": "def-cell-centered-rectangular-no-flux-tpfa-grid",
    "kind": "definition",
    "deps": [
      "def-fv-tpfa-no-flux-diffusion-specialization",
      "def-periodic-reaction-diffusion-grid"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 3,
    "title": "Cell-centered rectangular TPFA grid with homogeneous no-flux boundaries",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "not-applicable"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "R. Eymard, T. Gallouët, and R. Herbin, Finite Volume Methods, updated author text, 2019",
          "url": "https://raphaeleh.github.io/PUBLI/bookevol.pdf",
          "locator": "Chapter 9 §§9.2–9.3, PDF pp. 38–47: orthogonal cell-centered TPFA fluxes and boundary-face conventions."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $\\Omega=\\prod_{j=1}^d(0,L_j)$, where $L_j>0$, and let integers $N_j\\ge2$ define $h_j=L_j/N_j$. The finite cell set is $I_h=\\prod_{j=1}^d\\{0,\\ldots,N_j-1\\}$, cell centers are $x_i=((i_j+\\tfrac12)h_j)_{j=1}^d$, and every cell has volume $m_h=\\prod_jh_j$. This is the uniform orthogonal Cartesian mesh; adjacent center segments are perpendicular to their shared faces. Take the scalar cellwise coefficient to be $\\lambda_K=1$ and give boundary faces homogeneous no-flux data, so they contribute no flux.\n\nFor a grid vector $v=(v_i)_{i\\in I_h}$, define the positive cell-centered stencil\n$$\n(A_h^Nv)_i=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\frac{2v_i-v_{i+e_j}-v_{i-e_j}}{h_j^2},\n$$\nwhere an unavailable neighbor at a boundary is assigned the value of the boundary cell itself. Indeed a face normal to direction $j$ has area $|F|=m_h/h_j$ and adjacent centers are distance $h_j$ apart, so its TPFA transmissibility is $|F|/h_j=m_h/h_j^2$; dividing the cell balance by $m_h$ gives the displayed coefficient. Boundary faces contribute zero flux. Thus $M_h=m_hI$ and $L_h^N=m_hA_h^N$ are the mass and stiffness matrices of the no-flux specialization in [[def-fv-tpfa-no-flux-diffusion-specialization]]. For species diffusivities $d_r\\ge0$, the finite-volume diffusion equation is $U_r'=-d_rA_h^NU_r$.\n\nDefine the doubled periodic grid with $2N_j$ cells per direction on side lengths $2L_j$, using the same widths $h_j$, as in [[def-periodic-reaction-diffusion-grid]]. The reflection map $E_h$ extends $U$ evenly across every boundary face: in one coordinate, its extended index is $i$ for $0\\le i<N_j$ and $2N_j-1-i$ for $N_j\\le i<2N_j$; apply this coordinatewise. This finite reflection convention is used in the error theorem below."
  },
  {
    "id": "thm-reaction-diffusion-continuous-mass-balance",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "deps": [
      "def-semilinear-reaction-diffusion-system",
      "def-stoichiometric-mass-invariant",
      "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles",
      "thm-ftc-second-part",
      "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains",
      "def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain",
      "def-countable-choice"
    ],
    "dependency_level": 3,
    "title": "Continuous reaction-diffusion mass balance on boxes, tori, and piecewise C1 domains",
    "justified_by": [],
    "provenance": {
      "statement": "ai-altered",
      "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
      "references": [
        {
          "title": "Willem Hundsdorfer, Numerical Solution of Advection-Diffusion-Reaction Equations, lecture notes for Ph.D. course, 1996",
          "url": "https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/4797/4797D.pdf",
          "locator": "§0, printed p. 1: mass-balance modeling for multiple species; the local proof supplies the continuous no-flux and periodic identities."
        }
      ]
    },
    "statement": "Let $u\\in C^1([0,T];C^2(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb R^m))$ solve $u_t=D\\Delta u+F(u)$, with constant diagonal $D=\\operatorname{diag}(d_i)$, and let its range lie in $R$. Let $c$ be a conservation or dissipation vector on $R$. One may take (i) a box $\\Omega=\\prod_{j=1}^d[0,L_j]$ with periodic identification or homogeneous Neumann data, or (ii) a bounded domain $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge2$, with a specified finite piecewise $C^1$ boundary presentation as in [[def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain]], assuming $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ in this second branch and homogeneous Neumann data on every face outside the edge set. For either branch,\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_\\Omega c^Tu(x,t)\\,dx=\\int_\\Omega c^TF(u(x,t))\\,dx.\n$$\nHence the total mass is constant when $c^TF=0$ on $R$ and nonincreasing when $c^TF\\le0$ on $R$. The first branch is choice-free; $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used only for the stated piecewise $C^1$ divergence-theorem branch."
  },
  {
