        {
          "id": "cex-delaunay-tetrahedral-sliver",
          "kind": "counterexample",
          "title": "An empty-sphere tetrahedron can be arbitrarily flat",
          "deps": [
            "def-three-dimensional-delaunay-and-weighted-regular-lower-hull"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "For each integer $n\\ge1$, set $\\varepsilon_n=2^{-n}$ and take the four unweighted sites with distinct stable identifiers $1,2,3,4$ at $p_1=(0,0,0)$, $p_2=(1,0,0)$, $p_3=(0,1,0)$, and $p_4=(1/4,1/4,\\varepsilon_n)$. All coordinates are dyadic. They span one tetrahedron. Its circumsphere is empty of other sites because there are no other sites, so it is a Delaunay tetrahedron, while its inradius tends to zero and its diameter is at least $1$ as $n\\to\\infty$.",
