  {
    "id": "def-hp-variable-degree-spaces-on-matching-meshes",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Variable-degree conforming hp spaces on matching affine meshes",
    "deps": [
      "def-matching-affine-simplicial-meshes"
    ],
    "justified_by": [],
    "dependency_level": 0,
    "statement": "Let $d\\ge2$ and let $\\mathcal T$ be a finite matching affine simplicial mesh of a bounded polyhedral open set $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ in the sense of [[def-matching-affine-simplicial-meshes]]. A **degree map** is a function $p:\\mathcal T\\to\\mathbb N_0$ with finite values. The broken simplex space associated with $(\\mathcal T,p)$ is\n$$\nV^{\\mathrm{br}}(\\mathcal T,p):=\\{v\\in L^2(\\Omega):v|_K\\in\\mathbb P_{p_K}(K)\\text{ for every }K\\in\\mathcal T\\},\n$$\nwhere $p_K:=p(K)$ and $\\mathbb P_m(K)$ denotes restrictions of real polynomials of total degree at most $m$. Its **conforming hp space** is the subspace of functions whose two polynomial traces agree on every interior facet. The zero-boundary hp space further requires the polynomial trace to vanish on every boundary facet. For an interior facet $F=K\\cap K'$, its trace degree is $p_F:=\\min(p_K,p_{K'})$; equality of the two traces means that their common polynomial belongs to $\\mathbb P_{p_F}(F)$.\n\nA **matching affine tensor-box mesh** is a finite face-to-face partition of a bounded polytope into affine images of $[-1,1]^d$, with pairwise disjoint interiors. For a scalar degree $p_K$ on each box, the local tensor space is $\\mathbb Q_{p_K}(K)$, the pullback of polynomials of degree at most $p_K$ in each reference coordinate. The conforming box hp space is defined by equality of polynomial traces across every shared face, and its zero-boundary subspace by zero traces on the exterior faces. For a shared face $F=K\\cap K'$, set $p_F=\\min(p_K,p_{K'})$; its common trace lies in $\\mathbb Q_{p_F}(F)$ in tangential reference coordinates. This definition of tensor boxes does not include curved or non-affine cells.\n\nOn either mesh, **h-refinement** replaces one cell by a finite matching partition of that cell and assigns degrees to the children; **p-refinement** raises at least one cell degree while leaving the mesh fixed; **hp-refinement** performs both operations. A local polynomial basis is **hierarchical** if its ordered degree layers span the nested spaces $\\mathbb P_0\\subset\\cdots\\subset\\mathbb P_p$ (or $\\mathbb Q_0\\subset\\cdots\\subset\\mathbb Q_p$), with each new layer extending the preceding span. For example, pullbacks of reference monomials $\\widehat x^\\nu$ ordered by $|\\nu|$ give such a basis for $\\mathbb P_p$, and tensor monomials $\\xi^\\nu$ ordered by $\\max_i\\nu_i$ give one for $\\mathbb Q_p$. This basis convention does not assert stability of a particular basis or projector.",
