          "id": "thm-isogeometric-poisson-galerkin-quasioptimality",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Exact-geometry isogeometric Poisson Galerkin estimate",
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $D\\subset\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$, be bounded, connected, and Lipschitz, and let $V=H^1_0(D;\\mathbb R)$ with norm $\\|v\\|_V=\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2(D)}$. Let $A:D\\to\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ be measurable and symmetric, with constants $0<\\alpha\\le\\beta<\\infty$ such that\n$$\\zeta\\cdot A(x)\\zeta\\ge\\alpha|\\zeta|^2\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\|A(x)\\|_{\\rm op}\\le\\beta\\quad\\text{for a.e. }x\\in D,\\ \\zeta\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$\nFor $f\\in V'$, define $a(u,v)=\\int_D A\\nabla u\\cdot\\nabla v$ and $F(v)=f(v)$. Let $V_h\\subset V$ be any finite conforming isogeometric subspace on the exact admissible patch geometry. Then the weak problem and its Galerkin restriction have unique solutions $u$ and $u_h$, and\n$$\\|u-u_h\\|_V\\le\\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\inf_{v_h\\in V_h}\\|u-v_h\\|_V.$$\nThis is an exact-form estimate; it makes no assertion about approximate geometry, quadrature, polynomial-degree-independent constants, or a rate without a separate numerator approximation theorem.",
