    "id": "thm-nurbs-tensor-product-l2-approximation",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "Tensorized spline quasi-interpolation gives a NURBS L2 rate",
    "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. On each coordinate interval $[0,1]$, let a clamped degree-$p_r$ analysis knot mesh have largest cell size $h_r$, and let $Q_r$ be a family as in [[def-local-spline-quasi-interpolant]] with reproduction order $0\\le\\ell_r\\le p_r$, local constant $M_r$, and stability constant $C_r$. Require each analysis knot mesh to refine the corresponding geometry knot vector, and let $S_h=\\bigotimes_{r=1}^d S_{p_r,\\xi^{(r)}}$ be the corresponding numerator space defining the patch trial space in [[def-isogeometric-galerkin-patch-space-and-refinement]]. Suppose $g:\\widehat\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ belongs to $L^2(\\widehat\\Omega)$, has weak pure derivatives $\\partial_r^{\\ell_r+1}g\\in L^2(\\widehat\\Omega)$, and, for each $r$, almost every section in coordinate $r$ belongs to $W^{\\ell_r+1,2}(0,1)$ and satisfies the hypotheses of the one-dimensional estimate in [[thm-sobolev-spline-quasi-interpolant-rate]]. Require each $Q_r$ to have its bounded extension to $L^2(\\widehat\\Omega)$, acting in coordinate $r$, with norm at most $S_r=C_r\\sqrt{M_r}$; require the coordinate operators to commute pairwise and to commute with weak derivatives in every other coordinate on this class. These are explicit hypotheses on the supplied family, not conclusions of the univariate rate theorem. Under the Axiom of Choice, completed-product Fubini is available, and the sectionwise operators and derivatives are assumed measurable. Define $\\mathcal Q:=Q_d\\cdots Q_1$ and set $A_r:=(1+C_r)M_r^{\\ell_r+1+1/2}/\\ell_r!$. Then $\\mathcal Qg\\in S_h$ and\n$$\\|g-\\mathcal Qg\\|_{L^2(\\widehat\\Omega)} \\le\\sum_{r=1}^d A_r\\!\\left(\\prod_{j\\ne r}S_j\\right)h_r^{\\ell_r+1}\\|\\partial_r^{\\ell_r+1}g\\|_{L^2(\\widehat\\Omega)}.$$\nIf $g=W(\\widehat u)$ and $v_h:=(\\mathcal Qg/W)\\circ F^{-1}$, then\n$$\\|u-v_h\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{j_+}}{w_-}\\|g-\\mathcal Qg\\|_{L^2(\\widehat\\Omega)},\\qquad u:=\\widehat u\\circ F^{-1}.$$\nThis is an $L^2$ approximation estimate. It does not imply an $H^1$ energy rate.",
